window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("slm-for-agents", {"topics":[{"key":"slm-for-agents/when-an-slm-is-enough","sources":["Ollama documentation","vLLM documentation","Hugging Face model cards (Llama, Qwen, Phi, Gemma, Mistral)"],"why":"- **Agents multiply calls.** A 10-step agent with a planner, tool calls and a summariser can make 15-30 model\n  calls per task. A per-call price gap of 10x or more between a small self-hosted or budget model and a\n  frontier model becomes the whole margin (illustrative orders of magnitude; check current pricing).\n- **Latency compounds.** Sequential steps add up. A small model decodes faster and can run next to the tools,\n  so a chain of five classification or extraction calls stops dominating time to answer.\n- **Some data cannot leave.** Health records, source code under contract, or on-premises customer data rule\n  out a hosted API. A model you run yourself is the only option, and only small ones fit the hardware.\n- **\"Always use the biggest model\" fails quietly.** It works in the demo, then cost per task and p95\n  latency block the rollout; teams then downgrade blindly instead of per step with evidence.","how":"### The four questions, per step\n| Question | SLM is a good fit when | SLM is a poor fit when |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Task fit** | Output is a label, a short extraction, a fixed JSON shape, or a choice among few tools | Open-ended planning, multi-hop reasoning, long-horizon recovery from errors |\n| **Cost** | High volume of similar calls; you can amortise a GPU or use a cheap endpoint | Low volume, where a hosted large model costs less than running any server |\n| **Latency** | The step is on the critical path and needs fast, local responses | Latency is dominated by tools or humans anyway |\n| **Privacy** | Data must stay on device, on premises or in a region you control | The data is already allowed to go to a vetted hosted provider |\n\n### What makes a step \"narrow\"\n1. **Bounded output.** An enum, a schema, or a span copied from the input, so a validator can check it.\n2. **Knowledge comes from context.** The answer is in the prompt or the retrieved chunk, not in the model's\n   memory, because small models store far fewer facts and hallucinate them confidently.\n3. **Few choices.** A router over 3-8 intents or a step with 1-5 tools, because tool-selection accuracy of\n   small models drops faster than large ones as the tool list grows.\n4. **Short reasoning.** One or two inferential hops, not a plan with backtracking.\n\n### The decision procedure\n1. **Log real traces** and group the calls by step type (router, extractor, tool filler, summariser, planner).\n2. **Build a golden set per step**: 50-200 real inputs with expected outputs or a checker.\n3. **Run a small candidate and the current large model** on the same set, with the same prompt and\n   validation.\n4. **Accept the SLM for a step** when its pass rate is within your tolerance of the large model (say within\n   2-3 points) and cost or latency improves enough to matter.\n5. **Keep a fallback**: on schema failure or low confidence, escalate that call to the large model.","example":"### Per-step eval: is the SLM good enough for the router step?\nBoth models sit behind OpenAI-compatible endpoints (here Ollama locally and any hosted model), so one\nharness compares them. Replace the model names with your candidates.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nslm = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\")  # Ollama's OpenAI-compatible API\nbig = OpenAI()                                                          # hosted model, key from env\nINTENTS = [\"billing\", \"bug_report\", \"account_access\", \"other\"]\nPROMPT = f\"Classify the ticket into one of {INTENTS}. Reply with the label only.\"\n\ndef label(client, model, text):\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=model, temperature=0, messages=[\n        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": PROMPT}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": text}])\n    return r.choices[0].message.content.strip().strip(\".\").lower()   # tolerate \"Billing.\"\n\ndef pass_rate(client, model, cases):\n    ok = sum(label(client, model, c[\"text\"]) == c[\"expected\"] for c in cases)\n    return ok / len(cases)\n\ncases = [json.loads(l) for l in open(\"router_golden.jsonl\")]   # {\"text\": ..., \"expected\": ...}\nsmall, large = pass_rate(slm, \"qwen2.5:3b\", cases), pass_rate(big, \"YOUR_LARGE_MODEL\", cases)\nprint(f\"SLM {small:.1%}  large {large:.1%}\")\nprint(\"route to SLM\" if large - small <= 0.03 else \"keep large model for this step\")\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a support agent, step by step\nA support agent makes five kinds of calls per ticket. The team ran each step's golden set on a 3B-8B model\nand on their frontier model (figures are illustrative of the shape, not benchmarks).\n\n| Step | Calls per ticket | SLM pass vs large | Decision |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Intent router (4 labels) | 1 | 97% vs 98% | SLM |\n| Extract order ID and email | 1 | 99% vs 99% | SLM, schema-validated |\n| Fill `lookup_order` arguments | 2 | 95% vs 98% | SLM with fallback on invalid JSON |\n| Summarise tool output for the user | 1 | 90% vs 96% (judge-rated) | Keep large |\n| Multi-step refund policy reasoning | 1 | 71% vs 93% | Keep large |\n\nFour of six calls move small. If small calls cost a tenth of large ones, model spend per ticket falls by\nroughly 60%, while the two hardest calls keep frontier quality.","patterns":["**Split by step, not by agent.** Tag every model call in traces with a step name, then make the SLM decision per tag. Monitor pass rate and escalation rate per step on a dashboard, because one agent usually has both SLM-friendly and SLM-hostile steps.","**Validate, then escalate.** Wrap SLM steps in a schema or enum check; on failure, retry once and then call the large model. Alert when the escalation rate for a step rises above the rate you priced in.","**Gate with a golden set in CI.** Re-run each step's golden set when you change the prompt, the model or the quantization. Block the change if the pass rate drops more than your tolerance.","**Price the server, not the token.** For self-hosting, compute cost per task from GPU hours at real utilisation, not from free local tokens. Below a certain volume a hosted small or large model is cheaper.","**Keep private steps local by policy.** Mark steps that touch regulated fields and route them only to self-hosted models, enforced in the gateway rather than by convention."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent makes many model calls per task: routing, extraction, tool-argument\n  filling, summarising tool output, final answer. Most of them are narrow, and paying frontier prices for all\n  of them is the usual reason an agent is too slow or too expensive to ship.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A second model entry in your gateway or LiteLLM config, a local\n  endpoint (Ollama, llama.cpp, vLLM) for private data, and a router that sends easy steps small and hard\n  steps large.\n- **What you control.** Which steps go small, the acceptance threshold per step, the fallback when the SLM\n  fails validation, and the eval set that proves it.\n- **What you can skip.** Leaderboard debates. The decision rests on your traces and your golden set, not on\n  a public benchmark average.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Support and triage agents.** Routing, entity extraction and tool-argument filling run on an SLM, while\n  policy reasoning and customer-facing replies stay on a large model.\n- **Regulated data.** Hospitals, banks and legal teams run every agent step on a self-hosted small model\n  because records cannot leave their network.\n- **High-volume back-office extraction.** Invoice, form and email extraction agents process thousands of\n  documents a day where per-call cost decides feasibility.\n- **Latency-critical steps.** Voice and in-product assistants use a local SLM for intent and slot filling so\n  the first response arrives quickly.","mini_project":"**Per-step SLM audit of an existing agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** decide, with evidence, which steps of one of your agents can run on a small model.\n- **Steps:** log 100 runs of an agent and tag each model call by step; build a 50-case golden set for the two\n  highest-volume steps; run a local 3B-8B model via Ollama and your current large model on both sets with the\n  same prompt and validator; compute pass rate, p50 latency and cost per task for each.\n- **Done when:** you have a table per step with pass rates, the tolerance you chose, a route-small or\n  keep-large decision, and an estimate of savings including escalation cost."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Agent step from traces\"] --> P{\"Data may leave your boundary?\"}\n    P -->|\"no\"| L[\"Must self-host: SLM or nothing\"]\n    P -->|\"yes\"| N{\"Narrow, checkable output?\"}\n    N -->|\"no\"| B[\"Keep the large model\"]\n    N -->|\"yes\"| E[\"Eval SLM vs large on golden set\"]\n    L --> E\n    E --> G{\"Within tolerance and cheaper or faster?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| B\n    G -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Route to SLM with validator\"]\n    R -.->|\"invalid or low confidence\"| B","caption":"Read it top to bottom for one agent step: hard constraints first, then evidence from your own eval, then a\nfallback so a wrong \"yes\" is cheap."},"interview":[{"q":"How do you decide whether a small model is good enough for an agent step?","a":"- **Characterise the step first.** Bounded output, knowledge in context, few choices and short reasoning\n  make it a candidate; open planning and fact recall do not.\n- **Then measure on your data.** Run the SLM and the incumbent on a golden set built from production traces\n  and accept within a stated tolerance.\n- **Ship it with a fallback** so a wrong decision costs an escalation, not a user-facing error."},{"q":"A customer cannot send data to a hosted API. How does that change your agent design?","a":"- **Every model call must run in their boundary,** so model size is capped by the hardware they provide,\n  which usually means 3B-14B models, quantized.\n- **Narrow the steps** to suit small models: retrieval for facts, fewer tools per step, schema-constrained\n  output, and deterministic code wherever a model is not needed.\n- **Eval on their data on site** before promising quality, since the gap to a frontier model varies by task."},{"q":"When would you not use an SLM even though it is cheaper per token?","a":"- **Low volume.** A dedicated GPU idling most of the day can cost more per task than a hosted large model.\n- **High escalation.** If 30% or more of calls fail validation and retry on the large model, you pay twice.\n- **Hard steps.** Planning over many tools or long-horizon recovery is where quality loss causes failed tasks,\n  which cost more than tokens."},{"q":"Your SLM router passed the eval but production escalations doubled after a month. What happened?","a":"- **Likely drift:** new products, intents or phrasing absent from the golden set; check the escalated inputs.\n- **Fix the loop:** sample escalated and low-confidence cases into the golden set, relabel, re-evaluate, and\n  either update the prompt or fine-tune.\n- **Guard it:** alert on escalation rate per step so the next drift is caught in days, not a month."},{"q":"How would you estimate the savings of moving steps to an SLM before building anything?","a":"- **From traces:** count calls and tokens per step type, then price each step at the large and small rate.\n- **Add escalation:** multiply the expected escalation rate by the large-model cost for those calls.\n- **Add fixed cost:** for self-hosting, GPU hours at realistic utilisation plus operations time, compared\n  against the per-task saving at current and forecast volume."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cost and latency vs headroom.** An SLM is cheaper and faster on the steps it passes, but has less margin for unexpected inputs. This flips when input distribution drifts, so monitor escalation rate.","**Privacy vs operations.** Self-hosting keeps data in your boundary, but you now own GPUs, upgrades, scaling and on-call. For low volume a hosted provider with the right data agreement may be the better call.","**More models vs simpler system.** Per-step routing saves money but adds a second model to evaluate, version and monitor. With only a few hundred tasks a day, one large model may cost less in engineering time.","**Fine-tuned SLM vs prompted SLM.** Fine-tuning can close the gap on a narrow step but adds a training pipeline and retraining on every schema change."],"pitfalls":["**Deciding from a public benchmark.** Symptom: the SLM looks strong on paper and fails on your tickets. Fix: decide only from a golden set built from your own traces.","**Asking the SLM to recall facts.** Symptom: confident wrong answers about policies or products. Fix: put the facts in context through retrieval, or keep that step on the large model.","**Moving the planner first.** Symptom: loops, wrong tool order and giving up early. Fix: move leaf steps (classify, extract, fill arguments) first; the planner is usually the last step to go small.","**Ignoring the fallback cost.** Symptom: savings vanish because 30% of calls escalate. Fix: include escalation rate in the cost model and only route steps where it stays low."],"when":"- **Use when** a step has a bounded, checkable output, high volume, a latency budget, or data that must stay\n  in your boundary.\n- **Avoid when** the step needs open-ended planning, broad world knowledge, or long tool lists, or when volume\n  is too low to justify any extra model.\n- **Measure** pass rate per step against the large model, escalation rate, cost per task and p95 latency.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"which parts of this agent would you move to a small model, and how would you know\n  it is safe?\"","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tags model calls by step, builds the per-step golden sets, and wires the validator and escalation path that makes an SLM safe to use.","architect":"Decides which steps may leave the data boundary, sets the acceptance tolerance per step, and owns the cost model that compares self-hosted SLMs with hosted models at real volume.","fde":"Explains to a customer with on-premises data which agent steps can run locally on a small model and which need a vetted hosted model, with eval numbers as evidence."},"check":{"q":"Your agent spends most of its budget on a frontier model. Its calls are: an intent router, an\nentity extractor, tool-argument filling, and a planner that chooses among 25 tools. Which would you move to an\nSLM first, and how would you prove it is safe?","a":"Move the router and the extractor first, then tool-argument filling, because they have bounded outputs\nthat a validator can check and they usually dominate call volume. Keep the planner on the large model: 25\ntools and multi-step choices are where small models degrade most. Prove it per step: build a golden set from\nreal traces, run the SLM and the large model on it with the same prompt, and accept the SLM only within a set\ntolerance. Ship with schema validation and escalation to the large model, then monitor escalation rate and\ncost per task, because a high escalation rate erases the savings."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which agent step is the best first candidate for a small language model?","options":["A planner that chooses among 30 tools and recovers from failed calls","An intent classifier that maps a ticket to one of five labels","A step that answers product questions from the model's own knowledge","A final answer that must combine results from six different tools"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A five-label classifier has a bounded, checkable output, needs no recalled facts, and is usually high volume. Planning over many tools, fact recall and multi-source synthesis are where small models fall furthest behind."},{"q":"What should decide whether an SLM replaces the large model for one step?","options":["Its average score on a public leaderboard","Its parameter count relative to the large model","Its pass rate on a golden set from your own traces compared with the large model, within a set tolerance","Whether it supports the same context window length"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Public benchmarks do not reflect your inputs, labels or tools. A per-step golden set run on both models with the same prompt and validator is the evidence that the swap is safe."},{"q":"Why can moving steps to a self-hosted SLM fail to save money?","options":["Small models always use more tokens per answer","Low volume leaves the GPU idle and a high escalation rate means many calls are paid for twice","Quantized models cannot run on GPUs","Self-hosted models cannot use prompt caching"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Self-hosting has a fixed cost that only pays off at enough volume, and every call that fails validation and escalates to the large model costs both the small and the large call."}]},{"key":"slm-for-agents/choosing-an-slm","sources":["Hugging Face model cards (Meta Llama, Qwen, Microsoft Phi, Google Gemma, Mistral AI)","Ollama documentation (model library)","vLLM documentation (supported models)"],"why":"- **There are hundreds of candidates.** Each family ships several sizes, base and instruct variants, and\n  many community quantizations. Without a filter, teams pick whatever trended last week.\n- **Hard constraints eliminate most options.** A license that forbids your use, a model that does not fit\n  in 8 GB, or a chat template without tool support removes a model regardless of its quality.\n- **Benchmarks measure someone else's task.** General averages say little about selecting one of your 6\n  tools with the right arguments, or extracting your schema from your documents.\n- **The choice is reversible only if you planned it.** An OpenAI-compatible endpoint and a golden set let you\n  swap models in an afternoon; hard-coding one model's prompt quirks does not.","how":"### Step 1: hard filters (pass or fail)\n| Filter | What to check | Why it eliminates |\n|---|---|---|\n| **License** | Model card license: Apache 2.0, MIT, or a custom community or terms-of-use license | Some custom licenses add usage policies, attribution or scale conditions your legal team must accept |\n| **Hardware fit** | Parameters × bits per weight ÷ 8, plus KV cache for your context, under your VRAM or RAM | A model that does not fit runs on CPU or swaps, and latency collapses |\n| **Context length** | Supported context versus your prompt (system prompt, tool schemas, history) | Tool schemas and transcripts eat context fast in agents |\n| **Tool and chat template** | Whether the chat template defines tool calls and roles you need | Without a tool template, the engine cannot parse tool calls natively |\n| **Engine support** | Listed in Ollama's library, convertible to GGUF, supported by vLLM | No runtime means no deployment |\n| **Languages and modality** | Languages and images you need | Small models often degrade fastest outside their main languages |\n\n### Step 2: the families, described by traits (verify on current model cards)\n| Family | Typical small sizes | Traits worth knowing | License to check |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Llama** (Meta) | 1B, 3B, 8B | Broad ecosystem support, many fine-tunes and quantizations | Custom community license with usage policy |\n| **Qwen** (Alibaba) | from under 1B to 14B and up | Wide size ladder, strong multilingual and coding variants | Mostly Apache 2.0, but not all sizes (Qwen2.5-3B shipped under a research license); check each |\n| **Phi** (Microsoft) | around 4B mini, 14B | Strong reasoning for the size from synthetic data; narrower world knowledge | MIT for recent releases; check each |\n| **Gemma** (Google) | 1B to 27B | Good quality per size, some sizes accept images | Gemma terms of use |\n| **Mistral** (Mistral AI) | 7B, 8B, small around 24B | Efficient, function-calling support in recent instruct models | Apache 2.0 for some, other terms for others |\n\n### Step 3: shortlist and test\n1. **Pick 2-3 candidates from different families** at the size your hardware allows, instruct variants only.\n2. **Run the same golden set** for each agent step you plan to move, with the same prompt, engine and\n   quantization you will deploy.\n3. **Score what agents need:** task pass rate, valid-JSON rate, correct-tool rate, p50 and p95 latency, and\n   peak memory.\n4. **Pick the smallest model that meets the bar,** because the smallest passing model leaves room for\n   context, concurrency and a cheaper GPU.","example":"### Screening a shortlist on your own tool-calling step\nPull the candidates with `ollama pull`, then score each on correct tool, valid arguments and latency through\nOllama's Python client. The model tags are examples; use the current tags from the Ollama library.\n\n```python\nimport json, time\nimport ollama\n\nCANDIDATES = [\"llama3.2:3b\", \"qwen2.5:3b\", \"phi4-mini\", \"gemma3:4b\"]   # check current tags\nTOOLS = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"lookup_order\", \"description\": \"Find an order by its ID\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"required\": [\"order_id\"],\n                   \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}}}}]\ncases = [json.loads(l) for l in open(\"tool_golden.jsonl\")]   # {\"text\": ..., \"order_id\": ...}\n\nfor model in CANDIDATES:\n    ok, lat = 0, []\n    for c in cases:\n        t = time.time()\n        try:\n            r = ollama.chat(model=model, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": c[\"text\"]}],\n                            tools=TOOLS, options={\"temperature\": 0})\n        except ollama.ResponseError as e:          # e.g. \"... does not support tools\"\n            print(f\"{model:14} hard-filter fail: {e.error}\")\n            break\n        lat.append(time.time() - t)\n        calls = r.message.tool_calls or []\n        ok += bool(calls) and calls[0].function.name == \"lookup_order\" \\\n              and calls[0].function.arguments.get(\"order_id\") == c[\"order_id\"]\n    else:                                          # runs only if no hard-filter break\n        lat.sort()\n        print(f\"{model:14} pass {ok/len(cases):.0%}  p50 {lat[len(lat)//2]:.2f}s\")\n```\n\nA model whose template lacks tool support either makes Ollama return an error (caught above) or answers in\nplain text with no tool call; both are hard-filter failures, not low scores.\n\n### Worked scenario: picking a model for an on-premises document agent\nConstraints: one 24 GB GPU, English and German, 16k-token prompts, commercial use, tool calls to 4 tools.\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| License review | Two candidates cleared quickly; one custom license went to legal and was parked |\n| Hardware | 14B at 4-bit fits weights but leaves little KV cache for 16k context at concurrency 4; 7B-8B fits comfortably |\n| Shortlist | One 7B-8B model from each of two families, plus a 3B as a cheap baseline |\n| Eval on 150 real documents | Both 7B-8B models within 2 points on extraction; one had clearly better German tool-argument filling |\n| Decision | The better German model, at a 4-bit or 5-bit quantization that kept the eval within tolerance |\n\nThe 3B baseline failed German extraction, which no general benchmark had flagged.","patterns":["**Keep a model registry entry per choice.** Record repo id, exact revision or file hash, quantization, license, chat template and eval scores. Re-run the golden set before any upgrade, because a new release of the same family can change tool-call formats.","**Abstract the endpoint.** Serve every candidate behind an OpenAI-compatible API (Ollama, llama.cpp server, vLLM) and call it through one client or LiteLLM, so swapping models is a config change.","**Re-screen on a schedule.** Re-run the shortlist each quarter or when a family ships a new generation, since a newer small model often matches an older larger one and frees hardware.","**Pin the chat template.** Use the template from the model's own repo; community GGUF files sometimes ship an older or broken template, which shows up as unparsed tool calls.","**Log model identity in traces.** Attach model name, revision and quantization to every span so a quality regression can be traced to a model change."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The model decides whether tool calls parse, whether JSON validates and\n  whether the agent fits your GPU or laptop. Two models with similar benchmark averages can differ sharply on\n  your tool-calling step.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The model tag in Ollama, the GGUF file for llama.cpp, the Hugging Face\n  repo id in `vllm serve`, and the chat template that formats tools for that model.\n- **What you control.** The shortlist, the size and quantization that fit your hardware, the license review,\n  and the eval that decides.\n- **What you can skip.** Exact benchmark numbers in blog posts. Families release new versions every few\n  months, so learn the selection criteria and re-run them on the current model cards.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **On-premises agents.** Teams pick the best model that fits the customer's single GPU and license policy,\n  then lock its revision in a registry.\n- **Multilingual assistants.** Screening reveals which family keeps tool calling accurate in the customer's\n  languages, which general benchmarks rarely show.\n- **Cost reviews.** A quarterly re-screen often finds a newer, smaller model that matches the current one and\n  frees hardware.\n- **Vendor-exit planning.** A shortlist of open models from different families gives a tested fallback if a\n  hosted provider changes terms.","mini_project":"**Build a model-selection scorecard (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** choose a small model for one agent step with a documented, repeatable process.\n- **Steps:** write the hard filters for your setting (license, memory, context, languages); pick three\n  instruct models from different families that pass them, using current model cards; pull them with Ollama;\n  build a 50-case golden set for one tool-calling step; run the screening harness and record pass rate, p50\n  latency and peak memory.\n- **Done when:** you have a scorecard table, the chosen model with its exact tag and quantization, and a short\n  note on why the runners-up lost."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Candidate models from current cards\"] --> L{\"License fits your use?\"}\n    L -->|\"no\"| X[\"Drop\"]\n    L -->|\"yes\"| H{\"Fits VRAM with your context?\"}\n    H -->|\"no\"| X\n    H -->|\"yes\"| T{\"Tool template and engine support?\"}\n    T -->|\"no\"| X\n    T -->|\"yes\"| S[\"Shortlist 2-3 from different families\"]\n    S --> E[\"Run your agent golden set\"]\n    E --> P[\"Smallest model that passes the bar\"]","caption":"Hard filters remove models before any testing; the eval picks among what is left, and the smallest passing\nmodel wins."},"interview":[{"q":"Why not just pick the small model with the highest benchmark score?","a":"- **Benchmarks measure other tasks.** Your step is a specific tool list, schema and language mix that general\n  averages do not cover.\n- **Hard constraints come first.** License, memory with your context, and tool-template support eliminate\n  models regardless of score.\n- **The deployed build differs.** Quantization and engine change behaviour, so you must test the exact build."},{"q":"What goes into your hardware-fit estimate for a candidate SLM?","a":"- **Weights:** parameters times effective bits per weight divided by 8, for the quantization you will use.\n- **KV cache:** bytes per token from the model config times context length times concurrent sequences.\n- **Headroom:** around 10% for activations and fragmentation; if it does not fit, step down in size or\n  precision and re-run the eval."},{"q":"A new version of your model family was released. How do you decide whether to upgrade?","a":"- **Re-run the golden set** for every agent step that uses the model, on the deployed quantization and engine.\n- **Check format changes:** tool-call format and chat template can change between generations and break\n  parsing.\n- **Upgrade only if it wins,** or matches at a smaller size, and roll out behind a flag with traces tagged by\n  model revision."},{"q":"How would you choose between a 3B and an 8B model for an agent's argument-filling step?","a":"- **Measure both** on the same golden set with validation; record pass rate, invalid-JSON rate and latency.\n- **Price the difference:** the 8B needs more memory per replica and serves fewer concurrent agents.\n- **Decide by the bar:** take the 3B if it passes with escalation to a larger model on failure, otherwise the 8B."},{"q":"What would you record about a model choice so the decision is auditable later?","a":"- **Identity:** repo id, revision or file hash, quantization, chat template source.\n- **Evidence:** golden-set scores per step, latency and memory on the target hardware, date of the run.\n- **Constraints:** license and legal sign-off, data-handling notes, and the fallback model."}],"tradeoffs":["**Bigger model vs headroom.** A 14B model may pass more cases, but on fixed hardware it leaves less memory for context and concurrent agents. The smallest model that passes usually serves more users.","**Permissive license vs best quality.** An Apache 2.0 or MIT model is easy to ship; a custom license may have the best scores but adds legal review and conditions. This flips when legal has already approved one.","**One family vs several.** Standardising on one family simplifies templates and tooling, but ties you to its release cadence and gaps, such as weak support for one of your languages.","**Specialist variant vs general instruct.** Coder or math variants win on their domain but can be weaker at general instruction following and tool use."],"pitfalls":["**Choosing from a leaderboard.** Symptom: a top-ranked model fails your tool calls. Fix: shortlist with hard filters, then decide on your own golden set.","**Testing a different build than you deploy.** Symptom: production quality is lower than the eval. Fix: evaluate the exact quantization, engine and template you will ship.","**Using a base model.** Symptom: rambling text and no tool calls. Fix: use the instruct or chat variant.","**Skipping the license.** Symptom: a late legal block before launch. Fix: put license review first in the checklist and record the outcome in the registry."],"when":"- **Use when** you have decided a step can run small and need a specific model for it, or when a family\n  ships a new generation and you want to know if it should replace yours.\n- **Avoid when** the step has not been shown to suit an SLM at all; settle that first.\n- **Measure** pass rate, valid-JSON and correct-tool rates, p95 latency and peak memory, per candidate, on\n  the deployed build.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"walk me through how you would pick a small model for this agent\" and a follow-up\n  on license and hardware.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs the screening harness across candidates, records scores per agent step, and pins the chosen model's revision, quantization and template in the registry.","architect":"Sets the hard filters (license classes, hardware envelope, languages) and the acceptance bar, and keeps the endpoint abstraction that makes models swappable.","fde":"Runs the shortlist on the customer's own documents and hardware and explains why the smallest passing model is the right one for their constraints."},"check":{"q":"A team must pick a small model for a tool-calling agent on a 16 GB GPU, for commercial use in English\nand Spanish, with 8k-token prompts. How should they choose?","a":"Start with hard filters: license that allows their commercial use, a size and quantization whose\nweights plus KV cache for 8k tokens at their concurrency fit in 16 GB (typically 3B-8B at 4-5 bits), a chat\ntemplate with tool support, and engine support in their runtime. Then shortlist two or three instruct models\nfrom different families, for example Llama, Qwen, Phi, Gemma or Mistral, checked on current model cards. Run\nthe same golden set of real tool-calling and Spanish cases on the exact build they will deploy, scoring\ncorrect tool, valid arguments and latency. Pick the smallest model that meets the bar and record its revision\nand template."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which check should eliminate a candidate SLM before any quality testing?","options":["It ranks below another model on a public leaderboard","Its weights plus KV cache for your context do not fit in the target GPU memory","It comes from a different family than your current model","It has fewer parameters than your current model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hardware fit, license and tool-template support are hard filters: a model that does not fit runs slowly or not at all. Leaderboard rank and family are not reasons to drop a model before your own eval."},{"q":"Why should you evaluate the exact quantization and engine you will deploy?","options":["Engines refuse to load models that were evaluated elsewhere","Quantization, chat templates and engine parsing change tool-call and JSON behaviour, so another build can score differently","Quantized models always score higher than full precision","Model licenses require it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A 4-bit GGUF in llama.cpp and a bf16 model in vLLM are different artefacts with possibly different templates and parsers. Scores transfer poorly between them, especially for structured output."},{"q":"Two candidates both pass your golden set; one is 3B and one is 8B. What is the usual choice and why?","options":["The 8B, because larger models are always safer","The 3B, because the smallest passing model leaves more memory for context and concurrent agents and costs less","Neither, because small models should not be used in agents","Whichever has the longer context window, regardless of fit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When both meet the bar on your tasks, the smaller model serves more agents per GPU at lower latency. Keep an escalation path for cases it fails."}]},{"key":"slm-for-agents/running-slms-locally","sources":["Ollama documentation","llama.cpp documentation (GitHub repository and llama-server README)","vLLM documentation"],"why":"- **Hosted APIs are not always allowed or affordable.** Regulated data, air-gapped sites and high call volume\n  push SLM steps onto hardware you run.\n- **Raw model weights are not a service.** You need tokenisation, chat templates, batching, a KV cache and an\n  HTTP API; these three engines package all of that.\n- **Agents need tool calls parsed, not just text.** Each engine can turn the model's tool-call tokens into\n  structured `tool_calls` in the response, if you enable the right template or parser.\n- **One size does not fit all.** A laptop, a CPU-only edge box and an 80 GB GPU serving 50 agents need\n  different engines, but the same OpenAI-style API keeps the agent code identical.","how":"### The three engines at a glance\n| | Ollama | llama.cpp (`llama-server`) | vLLM |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Built for** | Easy local use, model management | Portable C/C++ inference, CPU and GPU, edge | High-throughput GPU serving |\n| **Model format** | GGUF, pulled from its library or imported | GGUF | Hugging Face weights, plus AWQ, GPTQ, FP8 and others |\n| **Core abstraction** | Model tag plus Modelfile (`FROM`, `PARAMETER`, `SYSTEM`) | A GGUF file plus command-line flags | A model repo id plus engine arguments |\n| **Concurrency** | A few parallel requests per model | Fixed number of slots | Continuous batching, PagedAttention, prefix caching |\n| **Default port** | 11434 | 8080 | 8000 |\n| **Tool calls** | `tools` in its native and OpenAI-compatible APIs for models whose template supports them | Pass `--jinja` to use the model's chat template (explicitly, whatever your build's default) | `--enable-auto-tool-choice` plus a `--tool-call-parser` for the model family |\n\n### Starting each one\n```bash\n# Ollama: the server (desktop app or `ollama serve`) listens on :11434; pull a model into it\nollama pull qwen2.5:7b\nollama run qwen2.5:7b \"Say hi\"          # quick test; ollama ps shows loaded models\n\n# llama.cpp: serve a GGUF file on :8080, offload all layers to GPU, 8k context\nllama-server -m ./qwen2.5-7b-instruct-q4_k_m.gguf -c 8192 -ngl 99 --port 8080 --jinja\n\n# vLLM: serve Hugging Face weights on :8000 with native tool-call parsing\nvllm serve Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct --max-model-len 8192 \\\n  --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser hermes\n```\n\n### Settings that matter to agents\n- **Context length.** Ollama's default context is small and can silently truncate long tool schemas and\n  transcripts; set `num_ctx` (Modelfile `PARAMETER num_ctx 8192` or request `options` on the native API).\n- **Ollama's OpenAI-compatible endpoint has no per-request context setting,** so agents that use the OpenAI\n  SDK need the Modelfile or the server-wide `OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH`. In llama.cpp use `-c`, in vLLM\n  `--max-model-len`.\n- **Keep-alive.** Ollama unloads idle models after a few minutes; `OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE` or the request's\n  `keep_alive` avoids a cold load on the next agent step.\n- **Parallelism.** `OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL`, llama.cpp `--parallel` slots, and vLLM `--max-num-seqs` cap\n  concurrent requests; each one needs its own KV cache.\n- **Tool parser match.** The vLLM parser must match the model's tool format (for example `hermes`,\n  `llama3_json`, `mistral`); check the vLLM tool-calling docs for the current list.","example":"### A minimal tool-calling agent against any local engine\nThe same loop works with Ollama, llama.cpp (`--jinja`) or vLLM (tool parser on); change `BASE_URL` and\n`MODEL` only.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nBASE_URL, MODEL = \"http://localhost:11434/v1\", \"qwen2.5:7b\"   # or :8080/v1, :8000/v1\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=BASE_URL, api_key=\"local\")           # local servers ignore the key\n\ndef get_stock(sku: str) -> dict:\n    return {\"sku\": sku, \"in_stock\": 12}                         # stand-in for a real API\n\nTOOLS = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"get_stock\", \"description\": \"Units in stock for a product SKU\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"sku\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                   \"required\": [\"sku\"]}}}]\n\nmessages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"How many units of SKU A-17 are in stock?\"}]\nfor step in range(5):                                           # hard cap on agent steps\n    msg = client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, messages=messages,\n                                         tools=TOOLS, temperature=0).choices[0].message\n    if not msg.tool_calls:\n        print(msg.content); break\n    messages.append(msg)\n    for call in msg.tool_calls:\n        try:\n            result = get_stock(**json.loads(call.function.arguments))\n        except (json.JSONDecodeError, TypeError) as e:          # small models do emit bad args\n            result = {\"error\": f\"invalid arguments: {e}\"}\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call.id, \"content\": json.dumps(result)})\n```\n\n### Production setup: which engine where\nA team runs the same agent in three places. The comparison is qualitative; measure throughput on your own\nhardware and model.\n\n| Setting | Engine | Configuration that mattered |\n|---|---|---|\n| Developer laptops (16-32 GB unified memory) | Ollama | Pinned model tags, Modelfile with `num_ctx 8192`, one shared Modelfile in the repo |\n| Branch-office edge box, CPU only | llama.cpp `llama-server` | Q4_K_M GGUF, `-c 4096`, `--parallel 2`, started as a system service |\n| Central GPU server, 40 concurrent agents | vLLM | AWQ 4-bit 7B model, `--max-model-len 8192`, `--enable-prefix-caching`, tool parser matched to the model |\n\nAll three expose `/v1/chat/completions`, so the agent and its eval suite run unchanged in each place.","patterns":["**One client, many backends.** Route through LiteLLM or a thin gateway with entries for each local engine and a hosted fallback. Monitor per-backend error rate and latency so a failing local server shifts traffic instead of failing agent runs.","**Containerise with the model pinned.** Run the official Ollama or vLLM container images, or a llama.cpp build, with the model file or revision baked in or mounted read-only. Pin versions, because engine upgrades can change templates, parsers and defaults.","**Health check with a real completion.** Probe with a short chat request, not just an open port, because a server can be up while the model is still loading. Alert on time to first token and on queue length.","**Size concurrency from memory.** Set `--max-num-seqs` or `--parallel` from KV-cache arithmetic for your context length, then load-test at the concurrency your agents create. Watch preemptions in vLLM metrics.","**Trace local calls like hosted ones.** Send spans with model, engine, quantization, tokens and latency to Langfuse or your tracing backend, so SLM steps are visible in the same agent trace."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A local endpoint lets you develop agents offline, keep private data in your\n  boundary, and serve high-volume SLM steps without per-token bills.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A `base_url` such as `http://localhost:11434/v1` (Ollama),\n  `http://localhost:8080/v1` (llama.cpp server) or `http://localhost:8000/v1` (vLLM) in your OpenAI SDK,\n  LiteLLM or framework config.\n- **What you control.** The model and quantization, context length, how many requests run in parallel, and\n  whether native tool-call parsing is turned on.\n- **What you can skip.** Engine internals such as kernels and schedulers; `serving-inference/engines` covers\n  them. Here you need the commands, the flags that matter to agents, and when to switch engines.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Offline development.** Engineers build and debug agents on laptops against Ollama, with no API keys or\n  per-token cost.\n- **Air-gapped and on-premises agents.** llama.cpp or vLLM serve the agent's model inside the customer's\n  network.\n- **High-volume SLM steps.** A vLLM server handles classification and extraction calls for many agents,\n  while a hosted large model handles planning.\n- **Edge sites.** A CPU-only `llama-server` runs a small model in a store, factory or vehicle.","mini_project":"**Run one agent on three engines (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove your tool-calling agent runs unchanged on Ollama, llama.cpp and vLLM (or two of them if you\n  have no GPU).\n- **Steps:** pick one small instruct model with tool support; serve it with Ollama, then as a GGUF with\n  `llama-server --jinja`, then with vLLM and its tool parser if a GPU is available; run the example agent and\n  a 30-case tool-calling eval against each; record valid tool-call rate, time to first token and tokens per\n  second.\n- **Done when:** a table shows the three engines side by side, the agent code differs only in `BASE_URL` and\n  `MODEL`, and you can explain which engine you would deploy where."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Agent loop with OpenAI SDK\"] -->|\"base_url\"| R{\"Which engine?\"}\n    R -->|\"laptop dev\"| O[\"Ollama :11434\"]\n    R -->|\"CPU or edge\"| L[\"llama-server :8080\"]\n    R -->|\"GPU, many agents\"| V[\"vLLM :8000\"]\n    O --> G[(\"GGUF model\")]\n    L --> G\n    V --> H[(\"HF weights or AWQ\")]\n    O --> T[\"Response with tool_calls\"]\n    L --> T\n    V --> T\n    T -->|\"run tool, append result\"| A","caption":"The agent code stays the same; only the base URL changes, and each engine maps a model file or repo to an\nOpenAI-style chat endpoint that returns parsed tool calls."},"interview":[{"q":"When would you choose vLLM over Ollama for serving an agent's small model?","a":"- **Concurrency:** vLLM's continuous batching and PagedAttention serve many simultaneous agent sessions per\n  GPU; Ollama targets a few local users.\n- **Serving features:** prefix caching for shared system prompts and tool schemas, metrics, and fine control\n  over sequence and memory limits.\n- **Cost:** it needs a GPU server and more operational work, so it pays off only at real traffic."},{"q":"Your local model returns tool calls as raw text. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check the template:** the chat template must define tool calls; with llama.cpp start `llama-server` with\n  `--jinja`, with vLLM enable auto tool choice with the right parser.\n- **Check the model:** use an instruct model whose card documents tool support, at the deployed quantization.\n- **Add a fallback parser** only as a stopgap, and add the case to your eval set."},{"q":"How do you keep one agent codebase working across laptop, edge and GPU-server deployments?","a":"- **Same API everywhere:** all three engines expose OpenAI-compatible chat completions, so only `base_url` and\n  model name change.\n- **Config, not code:** keep engine, model, context and fallback in configuration or a gateway such as\n  LiteLLM.\n- **Eval per environment:** run the same golden set on each deployed build, since quantization can differ."},{"q":"How would you size a vLLM server for 40 concurrent agent sessions with 8k-token contexts?","a":"- **Weights first:** model size at its precision, for example a 7B at 4-bit.\n- **KV cache next:** bytes per token from the config times 8k times 40, plus about 10% headroom; set\n  `--max-model-len` and `--max-num-seqs` to match.\n- **Load-test:** measure time to first token and preemptions at 40 sessions before launch."},{"q":"What is the risk of upgrading the engine version under a running agent?","a":"- **Behaviour changes:** templates, tool parsers, defaults such as context or sampling, and quantization\n  kernels can change outputs.\n- **Mitigation:** pin engine and model versions, run the agent's golden set on the new build, and roll out\n  gradually with traces tagged by engine version."}],"tradeoffs":["**Ollama convenience vs control.** It is the fastest path to a working local agent, but exposes fewer serving knobs and lower concurrency than vLLM. It flips once you serve many concurrent users.","**llama.cpp portability vs throughput.** It runs on CPU, Apple silicon and modest GPUs from one GGUF file, but does not batch as aggressively as vLLM on a large GPU.","**vLLM throughput vs footprint.** Continuous batching and prefix caching give the most agents per GPU, but it targets GPU servers, pre-allocates most GPU memory, and has heavier dependencies.","**Local vs hosted.** Local keeps data in and per-token cost out, but you own uptime, upgrades and capacity."],"pitfalls":["**Truncated context in Ollama.** Symptom: the agent ignores tools or the system prompt on longer runs. Fix: raise `num_ctx` (Modelfile or `OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH` when you call the OpenAI-compatible endpoint) and confirm prompt token counts in the response usage.","**Tool calls returned as plain text.** Symptom: JSON-looking text in `content` and no `tool_calls`. Fix: start llama.cpp with `--jinja`, enable vLLM's auto tool choice with the right parser, or pick a model whose Ollama template supports tools.","**Cold starts between steps.** Symptom: some agent steps take seconds longer. Fix: set keep-alive or keep the model resident, and avoid loading several large models on one small GPU.","**Benchmarking at batch 1.** Symptom: fine in testing, slow under load. Fix: load-test with the real number of concurrent agents and long prompts."],"when":"- **Use Ollama when** you want a local model for development, demos or a single user in minutes.\n- **Use llama.cpp when** you need CPU or edge deployment, a single binary, or fine control over GGUF and\n  offloading.\n- **Use vLLM when** a GPU server must serve many concurrent agent sessions with high throughput.\n- **Measure** time to first token, tokens per second, valid tool-call rate and memory at your real\n  concurrency.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Starts the right engine with the right flags, points the agent's client at its base URL, and fixes context truncation and tool-parsing issues.","architect":"Chooses engines per environment, sizes GPU servers from KV-cache math and concurrency, and keeps all of them behind one OpenAI-compatible interface with a hosted fallback.","fde":"Installs a local model on a customer's machine or server, proves the agent works offline on their data, and explains when they will need to move from Ollama to vLLM."},"check":{"q":"Your agent works with a hosted model but, pointed at a local 7B model in Ollama, it ignores its tools\nafter a few steps and sometimes prints JSON as text. What do you check?","a":"First the context: Ollama's default context is small, so a long system prompt, tool schemas and a\ngrowing transcript get truncated; raise `num_ctx` and confirm prompt token counts in usage. Second, tool\nsupport: the model's template must support tools, otherwise tool calls come back as text in `content`; pick\na model whose template does, or move to llama.cpp with `--jinja` or vLLM with the matching tool parser.\nThird, robustness: validate arguments, return errors as tool results, cap steps, and run the agent's eval on\nthe local model before trusting it."},"quiz":[{"q":"What lets one agent client talk to Ollama, llama.cpp and vLLM without code changes?","options":["They all use the same model file format","Each exposes an OpenAI-compatible chat completions endpoint, so only the base URL and model name change","They all run on the same default port","They share one model registry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ollama, llama-server and vLLM all serve `/v1/chat/completions`. Formats and ports differ: Ollama and llama.cpp use GGUF, vLLM uses Hugging Face weights, and the defaults are 11434, 8080 and 8000."},{"q":"An agent on a local Ollama model starts ignoring its tools once the transcript grows. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The model has been unloaded and reloaded","The context window is set too small, so the tool schemas or system prompt are truncated","Ollama does not support tool calls at all","Temperature was set to 0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ollama's default context is small. When the prompt exceeds it, earlier content is cut, including tool definitions. Raising `num_ctx` and checking prompt token counts fixes it."},{"q":"Which setting is needed for vLLM to return parsed tool calls from a model?","options":["`--enable-prefix-caching`","`--max-model-len`","`--enable-auto-tool-choice` with a `--tool-call-parser` that matches the model's tool format","`--gpu-memory-utilization`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"vLLM parses tool calls only when auto tool choice is on and a parser matching the model family is chosen. Prefix caching, context length and memory utilisation are performance and capacity settings."}]},{"key":"slm-for-agents/quantized-slms","sources":["llama.cpp documentation (GGUF, llama-quantize, imatrix)","vLLM documentation (quantization)","Hugging Face Hub documentation (GGUF)"],"why":"- **Weights dominate small-model memory.** A 7B model in 16-bit is about 14 GB of weights before any KV\n  cache, too much for a 16 GB laptop or an 8 GB GPU.\n- **Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound.** Fewer bytes per weight means fewer bytes read per token, so 4-bit\n  models often decode faster than 16-bit ones on the same hardware, not just smaller.\n- **Agents need headroom.** Long tool schemas and transcripts need KV cache, and several concurrent sessions\n  multiply it. Shrinking weights frees memory for that.\n- **Naive rounding hurts.** Rounding every weight to 4 bits with one scale loses quality; block-wise scales,\n  k-quants and activation-aware methods keep most of it.","how":"### Memory arithmetic\n```\nweight_bytes ≈ parameters × effective_bits_per_weight ÷ 8\ntotal ≈ weight_bytes + KV cache (bytes_per_token × context × sessions) + ~10% overhead\n```\nEffective bits include the per-block scales, so a \"4-bit\" file is really around 4.5-5 bits per weight.\n\n### GGUF: the file format for llama.cpp and Ollama\n- **A container, not a method.** One file holds the weights, tokenizer, chat template and metadata, so a\n  model runs from a single download.\n- **Quant types are named in the file.** `Q8_0` (about 8.5 bits per weight) is near-lossless; `Q6_K` and\n  `Q5_K_M` are close; `Q4_K_M` is the common default; `Q3_K` and `Q2_K` save more and lose more.\n- **K-quants and IQ quants.** K-quants use blocks with nested scales; IQ types (for example `IQ4_XS`) reach\n  lower sizes at similar quality. Both benefit from an importance matrix.\n- **Importance matrix (imatrix).** A calibration pass records which weights matter on sample text, so the\n  quantizer spends precision there. Calibrating on text like your agent prompts is a cheap quality win.\n\n### AWQ: 4-bit weights for GPU serving\n- **Activation-aware.** It finds the small share of weight channels that see large activations and scales\n  them to protect them before quantizing the rest to 4 bits.\n- **Served by GPU engines.** vLLM loads AWQ checkpoints (published as Hugging Face repos with an \"AWQ\"\n  suffix) and runs them with fast 4-bit kernels, keeping continuous batching.\n- **Alternatives on GPU:** GPTQ (also 4-bit, error-compensating) and FP8 weights and activations on GPUs that\n  support it. Check the vLLM quantization docs for what your GPU supports.\n\n### Quality vs memory: the pattern to expect\n| Precision | Memory vs 16-bit | Typical effect on an SLM (illustrative; measure yours) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 8-bit (`Q8_0`, INT8, FP8) | about half | Usually indistinguishable on agent evals |\n| 5-6 bit (`Q5_K_M`, `Q6_K`) | about a third to two fifths | Small losses, rarely visible on narrow tasks |\n| 4-bit (`Q4_K_M`, AWQ, GPTQ) | about a quarter to a third | Small but measurable; structured output is the first to show it |\n| 3-bit and below | about a quarter or less | Often breaks JSON, arguments and multi-step reasoning in small models |\n\nSmall models tend to lose more from aggressive quantization than large ones, because each weight carries\nmore of the model's capacity. That is why a 4-bit 7B is often a better buy than a 2-bit 14B of similar size.","example":"### Sizing calculator: which builds fit your hardware\nEffective bits are approximate; read the exact file size from the model repo.\n\n```python\nBITS = {\"F16\": 16, \"Q8_0\": 8.5, \"Q6_K\": 6.6, \"Q5_K_M\": 5.7, \"Q4_K_M\": 4.8, \"AWQ-4bit\": 4.6}\n\ndef kv_gb(layers, kv_heads, head_dim, context, sessions, bytes_el=2):\n    return 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * bytes_el * context * sessions / 1e9\n\ndef total_gb(params_b, quant, **kv):\n    weights = params_b * BITS[quant] / 8                 # params in billions -> GB\n    return (weights + kv_gb(**kv)) * 1.1                 # ~10% overhead\n\n# A 7B-class model with GQA: 28 layers, 4 KV heads, head_dim 128 (read yours from config.json)\ncfg = dict(layers=28, kv_heads=4, head_dim=128, context=8192, sessions=4)\nfor q in BITS:\n    print(f\"{q:9} {total_gb(7.6, q, **cfg):5.1f} GB\")\n# F16 ~18.8 GB exceeds a 16 GB laptop; Q8_0 ~10.9 GB is tight next to the OS;\n# Q4_K_M ~7.1 GB and AWQ ~6.9 GB fit with room for four 8k sessions (KV cache ~1.9 GB).\n```\n\n### Agent eval gate across quantization levels\nRun the same tool-calling golden set on each build. Ollama publishes several quantizations of many models as\nseparate tags; check the model's tag list for exact names.\n\n```python\nimport json, ollama\n\nBUILDS = [\"qwen2.5:7b-instruct-q8_0\", \"qwen2.5:7b-instruct-q4_K_M\", \"qwen2.5:7b-instruct-q3_K_M\"]\nFMT = {\"type\": \"object\", \"required\": [\"order_id\", \"action\"],\n       \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                      \"action\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"refund\", \"replace\", \"escalate\"]}}}\nSYSTEM = \"Extract the order. Reply with JSON only, matching this schema: \" + json.dumps(FMT)\ncases = [json.loads(l) for l in open(\"extract_golden.jsonl\")]   # {\"text\", \"order_id\", \"action\"}\n\nfor build in BUILDS:\n    ok = 0\n    for c in cases:\n        r = ollama.chat(model=build, options={\"temperature\": 0}, messages=[\n            {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": SYSTEM}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": c[\"text\"]}])\n        try:\n            out = json.loads(r.message.content)\n            ok += out.get(\"order_id\") == c[\"order_id\"] and out.get(\"action\") == c[\"action\"]\n        except (json.JSONDecodeError, AttributeError):\n            pass                                        # invalid JSON counts as a failure\n    print(f\"{build:30} {ok/len(cases):.0%}\")\n# Decoding is left unconstrained on purpose, to expose how JSON validity degrades as bits drop.\n# In production, add format=FMT (constrained output) and measure field accuracy instead.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: fitting an extraction agent on 8 GB GPUs\nA team had to run an invoice-extraction agent on existing 8 GB GPUs (numbers illustrative).\n\n| Build | Fits 8 GB with 4k context? | Field accuracy | Valid JSON (unconstrained) | Decision |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 7B `Q8_0` | No | baseline | 99% | Rejected on memory |\n| 7B `Q4_K_M` with imatrix | Yes | 1 point below baseline | 98% | Shipped |\n| 7B `Q3_K_M` | Yes, more headroom | 6 points below | 91% | Rejected on quality |\n| 3B `Q8_0` | Yes | 5 points below | 97% | Kept as fallback candidate |\n\nWith constrained JSON decoding added, valid JSON reached 100% for the shipped build, and field accuracy\nbecame the only metric that mattered.","patterns":["**Default to 4-5 bit, prove it, then go lower only with evidence.** Start with `Q4_K_M` or `Q5_K_M` GGUF, or AWQ on GPU, and run the agent golden set against an 8-bit or 16-bit reference. Accept within a stated tolerance on task pass rate and valid-JSON rate.","**Calibrate with your own prompts.** When producing GGUF yourself, build an importance matrix with `llama-imatrix` on text resembling your agent's inputs, then pass it to `llama-quantize --imatrix`. Compare against the non-imatrix build on the golden set.","**Pin the exact file.** Record the repo, file name and hash of the GGUF or the revision of the AWQ repo, because community re-uploads with the same name can differ in template or quantization recipe.","**Spend saved memory on context and sessions.** After quantizing weights, re-run KV-cache sizing and raise context or concurrency deliberately. An FP8 or 8-bit KV cache (vLLM `--kv-cache-dtype fp8`, Ollama `OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE=q8_0` with flash attention on) roughly halves KV memory; re-run the eval after it.","**Monitor structured-output failure rate.** Track invalid JSON and rejected tool arguments per model build in traces; a rise after a quantization change is the earliest signal of damage."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Quantization decides whether your agent's model fits the hardware at all,\n  and how many agent sessions share one GPU. It can also quietly break JSON and tool arguments first.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** File names like `Q4_K_M` or `Q8_0` in GGUF downloads and Ollama tags,\n  AWQ or GPTQ repos loaded by vLLM, and the memory line in your capacity plan.\n- **What you control.** The format for your engine, the bit width, whether to use an importance matrix, and\n  the eval gate that accepts or rejects a quantized build.\n- **What you can skip.** The quantizer maths; `training-adaptation/quantization` covers GPTQ, AWQ and the\n  granularity ladder. Here you choose a file and prove it still works for your agent.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Laptop and desktop agents.** A 4-bit GGUF lets a 7B-8B model run locally next to the user's files.\n- **Edge devices.** Stores, vehicles and factory boxes run 1B-4B models at 4-5 bits within tight memory.\n- **Denser GPU serving.** AWQ or FP8 weights free memory for KV cache. Where 16-bit weights filled most of the\n  card (a 7B on a 24 GB GPU), that can roughly double the agent sessions it holds; on an 80 GB GPU the gain\n  is much smaller.\n- **Cost reviews.** Teams re-test lower bit widths when hardware is the bottleneck, gated on the agent eval.","mini_project":"**Find the smallest safe quantization for one agent step (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** pick the lowest-memory build of one model that keeps an extraction or tool-calling step within\n  tolerance.\n- **Steps:** pull three or four quantizations of one model in Ollama (for example 8-bit, 5-bit, 4-bit,\n  3-bit); build a 50-case golden set with expected JSON; run the eval gate script and record pass rate,\n  valid-JSON rate, peak memory and tokens per second for each.\n- **Done when:** you have a table per build, a chosen build with its exact tag, and one sentence on where\n  quality fell off."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    F[\"FP16 or BF16 weights\"] --> E{\"Target engine?\"}\n    E -->|\"llama.cpp, Ollama, CPU\"| G[\"Convert to GGUF\"]\n    G --> I[\"Optional imatrix calibration\"]\n    I --> Q[\"llama-quantize to Q4_K_M, Q5_K_M, Q8_0\"]\n    E -->|\"vLLM on GPU\"| A[\"AWQ, GPTQ or FP8 checkpoint\"]\n    Q --> V[\"Agent eval: JSON, tool args, task pass\"]\n    A --> V\n    V -->|\"within tolerance\"| S[\"Ship smallest passing build\"]\n    V -.->|\"fails\"| U[\"Step up bits or size\"]","caption":"Follow one model from full precision to a deployable file: the engine picks the format, and the agent eval,\nnot perplexity alone, decides which bit width ships."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between GGUF and AWQ?","a":"- **GGUF is a file format** for llama.cpp and Ollama that bundles quantized weights, tokenizer, template and\n  metadata, with many quant types from 2 to 8 bits.\n- **AWQ is a quantization method** that protects activation-salient channels and stores 4-bit weights,\n  typically served on GPUs by engines such as vLLM.\n- **Pick by engine:** GGUF for CPU, Apple silicon and edge; AWQ, GPTQ or FP8 for GPU serving at scale."},{"q":"How do you decide between a 14B model at 3 bits and a 7B model at 6 bits for the same memory?","a":"- **Do not guess:** both fit the budget, so run the agent's golden set on both builds.\n- **Expect** structured output to suffer more at 3 bits, because small and heavily compressed models break\n  JSON and arguments first.\n- **Choose** the one that passes with lower latency; often the higher-bit smaller model is more reliable."},{"q":"After switching to a 4-bit build, your agent's tool calls fail more often. What do you do?","a":"- **Measure:** compare invalid-JSON and wrong-argument rates against the previous build on the golden set.\n- **Mitigate:** add constrained decoding or schema validation with retry, try an imatrix or 5-bit build, or\n  route failures to a larger model.\n- **Prevent:** make the golden set a required gate for any quantization change."},{"q":"Why is a quantized model often faster as well as smaller?","a":"- **Decode reads all weights every token,** so it is limited by memory bandwidth, not arithmetic.\n- **Fewer bytes per weight** means fewer bytes moved per token, so tokens per second rise on bandwidth-bound\n  hardware, up to the point where dequantization overhead dominates."},{"q":"How would you size memory for four concurrent agent sessions on a quantized SLM?","a":"- **Weights:** parameters times effective bits per weight divided by 8, from the actual file size.\n- **KV cache:** bytes per token from the config times context times four sessions, plus about 10% overhead.\n- **Check** the total against device memory and leave room for the OS or other processes on shared devices."}],"tradeoffs":["**Memory and speed vs quality.** Fewer bits fit smaller hardware and decode faster, but each step down costs some accuracy, and structured output degrades first. The flip point is your eval tolerance.","**Bigger model at lower bits vs smaller model at higher bits.** At a fixed memory budget, compare both; a 4-bit larger model often wins, but below about 3 bits a smaller 8-bit model is usually safer.","**GGUF portability vs GPU throughput.** GGUF runs everywhere from one file but serves fewer concurrent requests than AWQ or FP8 in vLLM on a big GPU.","**Using a community quant vs producing your own.** Downloads are instant, but you inherit someone else's calibration data and template; producing your own costs an hour and gives control."],"pitfalls":["**Judging a quant by perplexity alone.** Symptom: perplexity barely moves but tool arguments break. Fix: gate on the agent's golden set, including JSON validity and argument accuracy.","**Comparing file sizes, not total memory.** Symptom: the model loads but crashes or swaps with long context. Fix: add KV cache for your context and sessions to the estimate.","**Mismatched format and engine.** Symptom: vLLM cannot load a GGUF efficiently, or Ollama cannot use an AWQ repo. Fix: GGUF for llama.cpp and Ollama, AWQ, GPTQ or FP8 for GPU engines.","**Going to 2-3 bits on a small model.** Symptom: loops, malformed JSON and wrong tools. Fix: step up to 4-5 bits or choose a smaller model at higher precision."],"when":"- **Use when** the model does not fit the target hardware at 16-bit, when you need more concurrent agent\n  sessions per GPU, or when decode speed matters on bandwidth-limited devices.\n- **Avoid when** memory is plentiful and the step is quality-critical, or below about 4 bits on small\n  models without a strong eval result.\n- **Measure** task pass rate, valid-JSON rate, tool-argument accuracy, peak memory and tokens per second for\n  each build.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"which quantization would you ship for this hardware, and how would you know it is\n  safe?\"","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks the GGUF or AWQ build for the engine, produces imatrix quants when needed, and runs the agent eval gate across bit widths before shipping.","architect":"Sets the memory budget per deployment, decides between larger-lower-bit and smaller-higher-bit options, and pins quantized artefacts in the model registry.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their 8 GB devices can run a 4-bit 7B model but not a 14B one, and shows the eval numbers that make the 4-bit choice safe."},"check":{"q":"You must run a tool-calling agent's 7B model on laptops with 16 GB of unified memory, with 8k-token\ncontexts. Which quantization would you start with and how would you validate it?","a":"At 16-bit the weights alone are about 14 GB, leaving nothing for the KV cache or the OS, so it does\nnot fit. Start with a `Q4_K_M` or `Q5_K_M` GGUF in Ollama or llama.cpp, which brings weights to about 4-5 GB\nand leaves room for 8k context. Validate by running the agent's golden set on that exact build and on an\n8-bit reference, comparing task pass rate, valid-JSON rate and tool-argument accuracy within a set\ntolerance. If it fails, try an imatrix build or `Q5_K_M`, and only drop to 3 bits with strong eval evidence.\nPin the file hash once accepted."},"quiz":[{"q":"Roughly how much memory do the weights of a 7B model need at `Q4_K_M` compared with 16-bit?","options":["About the same, because quantization only affects speed","About half","Roughly a quarter to a third, around 4-5 GB instead of about 14 GB","About a tenth"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`Q4_K_M` stores around 4.5-5 effective bits per weight including block scales, versus 16 bits, so a 7B model drops from about 14 GB to about 4-5 GB of weights. The KV cache still needs memory on top."},{"q":"Which agent metric usually reveals quantization damage first?","options":["Time to first token","Valid-JSON and tool-argument accuracy on the golden set","Number of tools defined","Length of the system prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Structured output is brittle: a slightly shifted token distribution produces a missing brace or a wrong field before broad answer quality visibly drops, so it is the canary to monitor."},{"q":"You need to serve many concurrent agent sessions on a large GPU with vLLM. Which quantized format fits best?","options":["A `Q2_K` GGUF file","An AWQ (or GPTQ or FP8) checkpoint that vLLM loads with its GPU kernels","Any GGUF file, since all engines prefer it","An unquantized FP32 checkpoint"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GPU engines are built around formats such as AWQ, GPTQ and FP8 with fast kernels and continuous batching. GGUF is designed for llama.cpp and Ollama, and 2-bit is too aggressive for agent reliability."}]},{"key":"slm-for-agents/tool-calling-and-structured-output-with-slms","sources":["Ollama documentation (tool support, structured outputs)","llama.cpp documentation (llama-server, grammars and JSON schema)","vLLM documentation (tool calling, structured outputs)","Pydantic documentation"],"why":"- **SLMs drift from formats.** Missing braces, extra prose around JSON, or tool calls written as text in\n  `content` appear far more often at 1B-8B than with frontier models, and more after aggressive quantization.\n- **Errors compound across steps.** At 95% per call, six calls succeed together only about 74% of the time\n  (0.95 to the 6th power), so per-call reliability decides task success.\n- **Prompting alone is not enough.** \"Reply only with JSON\" lowers the error rate but cannot guarantee it;\n  constrained decoding can guarantee syntax.\n- **Syntax is not correctness.** A perfectly valid call can still name the wrong tool or invent an ID, so\n  validation and checks against real data remain necessary.","how":"### Layer 1: native tool calling through the chat template\n1. You pass tool definitions as JSON Schema (`tools=[...]`).\n2. The engine renders them into the prompt with the model's chat template, the format the model was trained on.\n3. The model emits its tool-call tokens; the engine's parser turns them into `tool_calls` with a name and\n   arguments. In llama.cpp this needs `--jinja`; in vLLM, `--enable-auto-tool-choice` and a matching\n   `--tool-call-parser`.\n\n### Layer 2: constrained decoding for guaranteed syntax\n- **How:** the engine masks tokens at every step so only output matching a JSON schema or grammar can be\n  produced.\n- **Where:** Ollama's `format` parameter accepts a JSON schema; llama.cpp server accepts a JSON schema or a\n  GBNF grammar; vLLM supports JSON-schema `response_format` and further structured-output options (check the\n  current docs for names, which have changed between versions).\n- **What it guarantees:** parseable output with required fields, types and enum values. Not that values are\n  true.\n\n### Layer 3: validate, retry, escalate\n1. **Validate** with Pydantic: types, enums, ranges, and business checks such as \"order ID exists\".\n2. **Retry once or twice** with the validation error appended, because SLMs often fix a specific, named error.\n3. **Escalate** to a larger model when retries fail, and log the case for the eval set.\n\n### Designing tools for small models\n| Choice | For SLMs, prefer | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tool count per step | 1-5 relevant tools, chosen by a router or retrieval | Selection accuracy drops as the list grows |\n| Schema shape | Flat objects, few required fields | Nested objects and arrays are where small models slip |\n| Value types | Enums and constrained strings | Free text invites invented values |\n| Descriptions | One short, specific sentence per tool and field | Long descriptions consume context and blur choices |\n| Hard steps | Two stages: choose the tool, then fill its arguments | Each stage is a smaller decision |\n| Examples | One or two worked calls in the system prompt | Small models imitate examples closely |","example":"### Structured output from a local SLM with Ollama and Pydantic\nOllama's `format` takes a JSON schema and constrains decoding to it; Pydantic then checks meaning.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, ValidationError\nimport ollama\n\nclass RefundRequest(BaseModel):\n    order_id: str = Field(pattern=r\"^ORD-\\d{6}$\")\n    reason: Literal[\"damaged\", \"late\", \"wrong_item\", \"other\"]\n    amount_eur: float = Field(gt=0, le=500)\n\ndef extract(text: str, model: str = \"qwen2.5:7b\", retries: int = 2) -> RefundRequest | None:\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Extract the refund request as JSON.\"},\n                {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": text}]\n    for _ in range(retries + 1):\n        r = ollama.chat(model=model, messages=messages,\n                        format=RefundRequest.model_json_schema(), options={\"temperature\": 0})\n        try:\n            return RefundRequest.model_validate_json(r.message.content)\n        except ValidationError as e:                  # valid JSON, invalid meaning\n            messages += [{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": r.message.content},\n                         {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Fix these errors and reply again: {e}\"}]\n    return None                                       # caller escalates to a larger model\n\nprint(extract(\"Order ORD-123456 arrived smashed, please refund the 89.90 EUR.\"))\n```\n\n### Two-stage tool calling with escalation\nWith 20 tools, an SLM first picks the tool from an enum, then fills only that tool's schema. Both calls use\nJSON-schema `response_format` on an OpenAI-compatible server; support varies by engine and version, so\ncheck its docs.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nslm, big = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"x\"), OpenAI()\nTOOLS = {\"lookup_order\": {...}, \"cancel_order\": {...}, \"update_address\": {...}}  # fill: name -> JSON schema\n\ndef validate_against_backend(tool, args):            # stub: raise if IDs are unknown or not permitted\n    ...\n\ndef ask(client, model, prompt, schema):\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=model, temperature=0,\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n        response_format={\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"out\", \"schema\": schema}})\n    return json.loads(r.choices[0].message.content)\n\ndef plan_call(request, model=\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\"):\n    pick = {\"type\": \"object\", \"required\": [\"tool\"],\n            \"properties\": {\"tool\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": list(TOOLS)}}}\n    tool = ask(slm, model, f\"Which tool handles: {request}\", pick)[\"tool\"]\n    prompt = f\"Arguments for {tool} given: {request}\"\n    try:\n        args = ask(slm, model, prompt, TOOLS[tool])\n        validate_against_backend(tool, args)         # your checks: IDs exist, permissions\n    except Exception:                                # bad JSON or failed checks: escalate\n        args = ask(big, \"YOUR_LARGE_MODEL\", prompt, TOOLS[tool])\n        validate_against_backend(tool, args)         # escalated output is checked too\n    return tool, args\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: making a 3B agent reliable\nA field-service agent on a 3B model calls 12 tools. Rates per tool call on 200 golden cases (illustrative).\n\n| Change | Valid syntax | Correct tool and args | Task success (5 calls) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Prompt \"reply with JSON\", all 12 tools | 90% | 78% | about 29% |\n| + constrained decoding | 100% | 81% | about 35% |\n| + router narrows to 3 tools, flat enum schemas | 100% | 91% | about 62% |\n| + Pydantic checks, 1 retry, escalation on failure | 100% | 98% (2% escalated) | about 90% |\n\nConstrained decoding alone fixed syntax but barely moved task success; tool narrowing and validation did\nmost of the work.","patterns":["**Constrain everything the code parses.** Turn on JSON-schema or grammar decoding for every SLM call whose output is parsed, via Ollama `format`, llama.cpp JSON schema or vLLM structured outputs. Monitor invalid-output rate; it should be near zero, so any rise means a config regression.","**Validate against reality, not just the schema.** After Pydantic, check IDs exist, amounts are within limits and the user may act, before executing. Log rejected calls with the input for the eval set.","**Budget retries and escalate.** Allow one or two retries with the validation error, then call a larger model. Alert when the escalation rate per tool passes the level priced into the cost model.","**Narrow tools per step.** Use a router or embedding retrieval to show the SLM only the few tools relevant to the current step. Track correct-tool rate per step in traces.","**Gate changes on a tool-call golden set.** Re-run valid-call, correct-tool and argument-accuracy metrics when changing model, quantization, template, engine version or tool schemas."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Every tool call is structured output: a tool name plus arguments that must\n  parse and validate. A 3B-8B model that is 95% right per call fails roughly one task in four over six calls.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Ollama's `tools` and `format`, llama.cpp's `--jinja` and JSON-schema\n  grammars, vLLM's tool parsers and structured outputs, and Pydantic or Instructor on the client side.\n- **What you control.** How many tools the model sees, the schema shape, whether decoding is constrained,\n  the validator, the retry budget and the fallback model.\n- **What you can skip.** How grammar engines compile schemas into token masks; that is in\n  `serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation`. Here you apply them to SLM agents.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Local assistants calling internal APIs.** A small on-premises model fills arguments for ticketing, CRM\n  or inventory tools with schema-constrained output.\n- **Extraction pipelines.** SLMs turn emails, forms and invoices into validated records at high volume.\n- **Routers and dispatchers.** An SLM chooses the next tool or sub-agent from an enum, with a large model\n  handling escalations.\n- **On-device actions.** Mobile or desktop agents map user requests to app functions with a small model and\n  strict schemas.","mini_project":"**Harden a small-model tool caller (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** take a 3B-8B local model from naive tool calling to a validated, escalating tool caller and\n  measure each step's gain.\n- **Steps:** define 8-10 tools for a toy domain and a 50-case golden set of requests with expected tool and\n  arguments; run the baseline with all tools and prompt-only JSON; add constrained decoding; add tool\n  narrowing; add Pydantic and business checks with one retry and escalation to a larger model.\n- **Done when:** a table like the worked scenario shows valid-call, correct-tool, argument-accuracy and\n  escalation rates after each layer, with traces for the remaining failures."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"Agent step input\"] --> S[\"Select 1-5 relevant tools\"]\n    S --> M[\"SLM with tools and schema\"]\n    M --> C[\"Constrained decoding: valid JSON\"]\n    C --> V{\"Pydantic and business checks pass?\"}\n    V -->|\"yes\"| T[\"Execute tool\"]\n    V -->|\"no, retries left\"| R[\"Append error, retry\"]\n    R --> M\n    V -->|\"no, retries spent\"| L[\"Escalate to large model\"]\n    L --> T\n    T -->|\"result appended\"| U","caption":"One tool-call step with all three layers: the schema constrains syntax, the validator checks meaning, and the\nfallback catches what the small model cannot fix."},"interview":[{"q":"What does constrained decoding guarantee for a small model, and what does it not?","a":"- **Guarantees:** output that parses and matches the schema or grammar: required fields, types, enum values.\n- **Does not guarantee:** the right tool, true values or a sensible plan; those need validation against real\n  data and evals.\n- **Watch:** forcing structure too early can hurt reasoning, so give hard steps a reasoning field or a\n  separate call."},{"q":"How would you design tools for an agent that runs on a 3B model?","a":"- **Few tools per step:** a router or retrieval shows 1-5 relevant tools, since selection accuracy falls as\n  the list grows.\n- **Simple schemas:** flat objects, enums, short descriptions, one or two examples in the prompt.\n- **Safety net:** constrained decoding, Pydantic and business checks, one retry, then escalation."},{"q":"Your local model returns tool calls inside the text content instead of `tool_calls`. What is wrong?","a":"- **Template or parser:** the engine is not applying the model's tool template or not parsing its output.\n  Use `--jinja` in llama.cpp or the matching vLLM tool parser.\n- **Model support:** the model may not be trained for tools; check the model card or choose another.\n- **Interim fix:** constrained JSON output with your own tool-call schema, then validate."},{"q":"How do you decide between retrying the SLM and escalating to a large model?","a":"- **Retry when** the error is specific and fixable, such as a wrong enum or a missing field; one retry with\n  the error message usually works.\n- **Escalate when** retries fail, the step needs reasoning the SLM lacks, or the input is out of\n  distribution.\n- **Measure both rates** per step; a high escalation rate means the step does not belong on the SLM yet."},{"q":"How would you measure tool-calling reliability before shipping an SLM agent?","a":"- **Golden set of real requests** with the expected tool and arguments per step.\n- **Metrics:** valid-call rate, correct-tool rate, argument accuracy, retry and escalation rates, and\n  end-to-end task success, because per-call errors compound.\n- **Re-run** on every model, quantization, template, engine or schema change."}],"tradeoffs":["**Constrained decoding vs reasoning quality.** It guarantees syntax, but forcing JSON from the first token can reduce answer quality on harder steps. Add a short reasoning field first, or reason in a separate call.","**Retries vs latency.** Each retry adds a full SLM call; one retry usually fixes most errors, more rarely pay.","**Two-stage calling vs call count.** Splitting tool choice and arguments improves accuracy but doubles calls for that step; it pays off with many tools, not with two.","**Escalation vs privacy.** Falling back to a hosted large model rescues hard cases but sends that data out; for private data, escalate to a larger self-hosted model or to a human instead."],"pitfalls":["**Trusting syntax as correctness.** Symptom: perfectly formatted calls with invented order IDs. Fix: business validation against real data before execution.","**Exposing every tool every step.** Symptom: the SLM calls plausible but wrong tools. Fix: route or retrieve a small tool subset per step.","**Mismatched template or parser.** Symptom: tool calls arrive as text in `content`. Fix: correct chat template (`--jinja` in llama.cpp), matching vLLM parser, or a model whose template supports tools.","**Deeply nested schemas.** Symptom: missing inner fields and wrong array shapes. Fix: flatten, split into several calls, or let code assemble the nested structure from flat fields."],"when":"- **Use when** an SLM produces anything code consumes: tool calls, extraction results, routing labels,\n  structured plans.\n- **Avoid when** the output is free text for a human; constraining it adds nothing and may hurt quality.\n- **Measure** valid-output rate, correct-tool rate, argument accuracy, retry rate and escalation rate per\n  step.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"how would you make a small model call tools reliably?\" and the distinction between\n  syntax guarantees and correctness.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns on constrained decoding, writes Pydantic models and business validators, and wires the retry and escalation path around each SLM tool call.","architect":"Designs tool sets and schemas small models can handle, chooses where escalation goes under privacy constraints, and sets the reliability targets per step.","fde":"Shows a customer that a local small model can call their APIs safely, with validation logs and golden-set numbers as proof."},"check":{"q":"A 4B model in your agent emits valid JSON every time thanks to constrained decoding, yet 1 in 8\ntasks still fails at a tool call. What is going on and what do you change?","a":"Constrained decoding guarantees syntax, not correctness: the model is likely choosing a wrong tool or\ninventing or misreading argument values. Check failed traces to see which. Then narrow the tools shown per\nstep with a router, flatten schemas and use enums, add Pydantic and business validation against real data\n(IDs exist, limits hold), retry once with the validation error, and escalate to a larger model when retries\nfail. Measure correct-tool rate, argument accuracy and escalation rate on a golden set before and after, and\nconsider fine-tuning on logged tool calls if the gap persists."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does constrained (grammar or JSON-schema) decoding guarantee for an SLM's tool calls?","options":["That the chosen tool is correct for the request","That the output parses and matches the schema's structure, types and enum values","That argument values exist in your database","That the model will never need a retry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Token masking only allows outputs the schema permits, so syntax is guaranteed. Whether the tool and values are right still needs validation against real data and evals."},{"q":"A 3B agent sees 25 tools and often calls plausible but wrong ones. Which change helps most?","options":["Raising the temperature","Adding longer descriptions to every tool","Showing only the few tools relevant to the current step, chosen by a router or retrieval","Removing the JSON schema from the tools"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Small models' tool-selection accuracy drops as the list grows. Narrowing the candidate set per step makes each choice smaller and is usually the biggest single gain."},{"q":"Why can per-call reliability of 95% be too low for an agent?","options":["Because 95% means the model is too large","Because errors compound: over six calls the task succeeds only about 74% of the time","Because tool calls are always retried six times","Because constrained decoding lowers it further"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Independent per-call success multiplies across the steps of a task, so 0.95 to the 6th power is about 0.74. Validation, retries and escalation raise per-call reliability to make long tasks viable."}]},{"key":"slm-for-agents/fine-tuning-an-slm-for-an-agent-task","sources":["Hugging Face TRL documentation (SFTTrainer)","Hugging Face PEFT documentation (LoRA)","Hugging Face Transformers documentation (bitsandbytes quantization)","Hu et al., 2021 — LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","Dettmers et al., 2023 — QLoRA: Efficient Finetuning of Quantized LLMs"],"why":"- **SLMs are fluent but sloppy at agent formats.** Out of the box a 3B model may pick the wrong tool, invent\n  arguments or drift from the JSON schema on a few percent of calls, and an agent loop multiplies that\n  error across steps.\n- **Prompting has a ceiling.** Longer system prompts and few-shot examples help, but they cost context\n  on every call and stop improving a small model long before it is reliable enough.\n- **Full fine-tuning is too expensive to iterate.** Updating every weight of even a 7B model needs\n  multi-GPU memory for weights, gradients and optimizer state.\n- **LoRA and QLoRA make it a one-GPU job.** LoRA trains small adapter matrices (roughly 0.1-1% of\n  parameters, depending on rank and target modules); QLoRA also loads the frozen base in 4-bit, so a 7-8B model typically fits on one 24 GB card\n  (rough order of magnitude; check with your sequence length).\n- **What this buys.** A narrow agent step served by a cheap local model with lower latency and cost, and\n  your data stays in-house.","how":"### 1. Pick a narrow, measurable task\n- **Good targets:** intent routing, tool selection plus argument filling for a fixed tool set, structured\n  extraction, query rewriting for RAG, short grounded answers in a fixed format.\n- **Poor targets:** open-ended multi-step planning across many tools, or tasks needing knowledge the model\n  lacks. Fine-tuning teaches behaviour and format far better than facts; use retrieval for facts.\n- **Write the acceptance metric first** (for example exact tool name plus valid arguments), because the\n  same metric gates the release.\n\n### 2. Build the dataset\n| Ingredient | Why |\n|---|---|\n| Chat-format examples (`messages`, with `system`, `user`, `assistant`, `tool` roles) | matches how the agent calls the model at inference |\n| Your real tool schemas in the prompt, exactly as served | the model learns your names and argument types |\n| Coverage of every tool and of \"no tool needed\" | prevents over-calling a favourite tool |\n| Negative and edge cases: missing arguments, ambiguous requests, refusals | teaches asking or declining instead of guessing |\n| A held-out test split, never trained on | the eval gate needs unseen cases |\n\n- **Sources:** logged production traces (cleaned and consented), traces from a large-model agent (see\n  distilling-a-large-model-agent-into-an-slm), and synthetic variations.\n- **Size:** hundreds to a few thousand high-quality examples often beat tens of thousands of noisy ones for\n  a narrow format task (illustrative, not a rule; measure with a learning curve).\n- **Quality filter:** validate every tool call against its JSON Schema and drop examples that fail.\n\n### 3. Use the model's own chat template\n- Render training examples with the tokenizer's chat template (`apply_chat_template`), the same template the\n  serving engine uses, because a template mismatch silently breaks tool-call parsing at inference.\n- Train on assistant turns only when your trainer supports it, so the model is not trained to produce user\n  text or tool results. Recent TRL versions offer `assistant_only_loss=True` in `SFTConfig`, which needs a\n  chat template that marks assistant tokens; check the current docs for your version.\n\n### 4. Configure LoRA or QLoRA\n| Knob | Typical starting point | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| `r` (rank) | 8-32 | adapter capacity; higher fits more but overfits small data sooner |\n| `lora_alpha` | about 2x `r` | scales the adapter update |\n| `target_modules` | `\"all-linear\"` | adapting all linear layers usually beats attention-only for format tasks |\n| learning rate | around 1e-4 to 2e-4 | LoRA tolerates higher rates than full fine-tuning |\n| epochs | 1-3 | more epochs on small data memorises phrasing |\n| 4-bit base (QLoRA) | NF4 with bf16 compute | roughly quarters base-weight memory, small quality cost |\n\nThese are common starting points, not tuned values; sweep a few on your eval set.\n\n### 5. Train, evaluate, ship the adapter\n1. Watch train and validation loss; validation rising while train falls means overfitting.\n2. Run the held-out agent eval (tool accuracy, schema validity, task success), not just loss.\n3. Ship the adapter separately (vLLM can serve LoRA adapters on one base) or merge it into the base and\n   convert for llama.cpp or Ollama (check each tool's current conversion steps).","example":"### QLoRA fine-tune of a small model on tool-call traces\nA minimal TRL plus PEFT run. `train.jsonl` holds one `{\"messages\": [...]}` conversation per line, rendered\nwith your real system prompt and tool schemas.\n\n```python\n# pip install trl peft transformers datasets bitsandbytes accelerate  (versions move fast; check docs)\nimport torch\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, BitsAndBytesConfig\nfrom trl import SFTConfig, SFTTrainer\n\nBASE = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct\"     # any small instruct model; read its model card and license\nds = load_dataset(\"json\", data_files={\"train\": \"train.jsonl\", \"test\": \"test.jsonl\"})\n\nbnb = BitsAndBytesConfig(load_in_4bit=True, bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"nf4\",\n                         bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.bfloat16)        # QLoRA: frozen base in 4-bit\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(BASE, quantization_config=bnb, device_map=\"auto\")\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(BASE)\n\npeft_cfg = LoraConfig(r=16, lora_alpha=32, lora_dropout=0.05,\n                      target_modules=\"all-linear\", task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\")\nargs = SFTConfig(output_dir=\"out/router-lora\", num_train_epochs=2,\n                 per_device_train_batch_size=4, gradient_accumulation_steps=4,\n                 learning_rate=2e-4, logging_steps=10, eval_strategy=\"epoch\", bf16=True)\n\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(model=model, args=args, train_dataset=ds[\"train\"], eval_dataset=ds[\"test\"],\n                     processing_class=tok, peft_config=peft_cfg)   # uses the tokenizer's chat template\ntrainer.train()\ntrainer.save_model(\"out/router-lora\")   # saves the adapter only (a few MB to tens of MB)\n```\n\nDrop `quantization_config` for plain LoRA in bf16 when memory allows; it trains faster and avoids the\nsmall 4-bit quality cost.\n\n### Worked scenario: a ticket-triage agent step\nA support agent's first step picks one of 6 tools (or none) and fills arguments. A large hosted model does\nit well but costs too much at high volume. Illustrative figures from a typical project shape:\n\n| Run | Data | Tool + args exact match | Schema-valid |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 3B instruct, prompted with few-shot | none | ~70% | ~90% |\n| 3B + LoRA, 800 examples | real traces only | ~86% | ~99% |\n| 3B + LoRA, 2,500 examples | traces + synthetic \"no tool\" and missing-argument cases | ~94% | ~99% |\n| Large hosted model (reference) | prompted | ~95% | ~99% |\n\n- **What moved the number:** adding negatives and \"ask a clarifying question\" examples, not more epochs.\n- **Ship decision:** the fine-tuned SLM takes the step; low-confidence or invalid outputs escalate to the\n  large model (see slm-llm-cascades-and-routing).","patterns":["**Version data, base and adapter together.** Record the base model revision, dataset hash, chat template and LoRA config with each adapter, because a base-model update silently invalidates the adapter. Store them in your model registry and refuse to deploy mismatched pairs.","**Gate releases on the agent eval, not loss.** Run the held-out set plus a regression suite of past failures through the real agent loop and block the release if tool accuracy or task success drops versus the current model. Monitor the same metrics online per adapter version.","**Serve many adapters on one base.** With vLLM, start the server with LoRA enabled (`--enable-lora`) and register adapters per task or tenant, so one GPU hosts several fine-tuned agent steps. Check the current vLLM docs for adapter limits and flags.","**Refresh from production traces on a schedule.** Sample failed or escalated calls, have humans or a large model label the correct action, add them to training data and retrain, because tool sets and user phrasing drift.","**Keep a fallback path.** Route schema-invalid or low-confidence outputs to a stronger model, so a fine-tuning regression degrades cost rather than correctness. Monitor the fallback rate as an early alarm."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Prompting gets an SLM to maybe-correct tool calls; fine-tuning on your own\n  tool schemas and traces makes the format and the tool choice habitual, so you stop paying a large model\n  for a narrow, high-volume step.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Hugging Face TRL (`SFTTrainer`) with PEFT (`LoraConfig`), bitsandbytes\n  for 4-bit QLoRA, and hosted fine-tuning services; the adapter is then served by vLLM, Ollama or llama.cpp.\n- **What you control.** The task boundary, the training data (format, coverage, negatives), the chat\n  template, LoRA rank and target modules, learning rate and epochs, and the eval gate before release.\n- **What you can skip.** The low-rank math and optimizer internals; see training-adaptation/peft for those.\n  Know what rank, alpha and 4-bit loading cost you, and how to read a loss curve.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Intent and tool routing.** A fine-tuned 1-3B model picks the next tool or sub-agent for a support or\n  operations agent at a fraction of large-model cost.\n- **Structured extraction steps.** Pulling fields from emails, invoices or tickets into a strict schema\n  before an agent acts on them.\n- **Query rewriting for RAG.** A small model turns chat turns into search queries tuned to your index.\n- **Private on-prem agents.** Teams that cannot send data out fine-tune an open model on internal traces\n  and serve it on their own GPUs.","mini_project":"**Fine-tune a tool router and beat the prompted baseline (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make a 0.5-3B instruct model pick the right tool and arguments for a 4-tool agent more often\n  than the same model prompted with few-shot examples.\n- **Steps:** define 4 tool schemas; generate 600-1,000 chat examples (including \"no tool\" and\n  missing-argument cases) with a large model, validate them against the schemas, hold out the test split by\n  seed request before generating paraphrases; train a LoRA adapter with TRL; evaluate both models on 150 held-out cases.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of exact tool-plus-arguments match and schema validity for baseline vs\n  fine-tuned, and you can name the data change that moved the score most."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Define task and metric\"] --> D[\"Collect traces and synthetic cases\"]\n    D --> F[\"Validate against tool schemas\"]\n    F --> S[\"Train and test splits\"]\n    S --> L[\"LoRA or QLoRA training run\"]\n    L --> E[\"Agent eval on held-out set\"]\n    E -->|\"below baseline\"| D\n    E -->|\"passes gate\"| M[\"Merge or serve adapter\"]\n    M --> P[\"Canary in agent with fallback\"]","caption":"Data quality and the eval gate bracket the training run; the run itself is the cheap part."},"interview":[{"q":"How would you build the training set for fine-tuning an SLM to call your agent's tools?","a":"- **Start from real traces** in chat format with the exact served tool schemas, and validate every target\n  tool call against its JSON Schema.\n- **Cover the space:** every tool, \"no tool needed\", missing-argument and ambiguous requests, and refusals,\n  in proportions close to production.\n- **Hold out a test split by session** before any synthetic augmentation, so the eval measures\n  generalisation rather than memorised paraphrases."},{"q":"When would you choose QLoRA over LoRA, and what does it cost?","a":"- **Choose QLoRA when memory is the constraint:** the frozen base loads in 4-bit, so a 7-8B model fits on a\n  single consumer or 24 GB GPU.\n- **The cost** is slower training steps and a small quality loss from quantization; with enough memory,\n  bf16 LoRA is simpler and slightly better, so measure both on the agent eval."},{"q":"Your fine-tuned model emits perfect tool calls in tests but the agent loop breaks when served. What do you check?","a":"- **Chat template and parser:** the engine may use a different template or tool-call parser than training,\n  so the output text does not parse as a tool call.\n- **Stop behaviour:** if the model was trained on tool results, it may continue past the call and invent\n  output; check loss masking and stop tokens.\n- **Serving config:** confirm the adapter actually loaded and the base model revision matches."},{"q":"When should you not fine-tune an SLM for an agent?","a":"- **When the gap is knowledge, not behaviour:** use retrieval, because fine-tuning facts is slow to update\n  and hallucination-prone.\n- **When the task is broad or shifts often:** open-ended planning or weekly-changing tools make the data\n  stale, so a prompted larger model or a cascade is cheaper to maintain."},{"q":"How do you operate many fine-tuned agent steps in production?","a":"- **Serve adapters on a shared base** (for example vLLM with LoRA enabled), version each adapter with its\n  base and dataset, and route requests to adapters by task.\n- **Monitor per adapter** tool accuracy, schema validity and fallback rate, and retrain from labelled\n  failures on a schedule."}],"tradeoffs":["**LoRA vs full fine-tuning.** LoRA trains a tiny fraction of weights on one GPU and ships as a small file; full fine-tuning can reach slightly higher quality on large datasets but costs far more memory and produces a whole new model to host.","**QLoRA vs LoRA in bf16.** QLoRA fits bigger bases on smaller GPUs; it trains slower and adds a small quantization error. With enough memory, bf16 LoRA is simpler.","**Narrow specialist vs general SLM.** Fine-tuning boosts the target step but can degrade unrelated abilities (catastrophic forgetting); keep each adapter to one job and route other work elsewhere.","**Fine-tuning vs better prompting or retrieval.** Fine-tuning fixes format and behaviour; it is the wrong tool for fresh facts, which flips the decision toward RAG."],"pitfalls":["**Chat template mismatch.** Symptom: great eval in the notebook, garbled tool calls when served. Fix: train with the tokenizer's template and confirm the serving engine uses the same one and the same tool-call parser.","**Training on tool results and user turns.** Symptom: the model starts writing fake tool outputs. Fix: mask non-assistant tokens from the loss and test that the model stops after emitting a tool call.","**No \"no tool\" or ambiguous cases.** Symptom: the model calls a tool on every request. Fix: include negatives and clarification examples in proportion to real traffic.","**Test leakage.** Symptom: offline accuracy far above online accuracy. Fix: split by user or session before generating synthetic variants, so paraphrases of a test case never reach training."],"when":"- **Use when** one agent step is narrow, high-volume and measurable, a prompted SLM is close but not\n  reliable, and you can collect a few hundred to a few thousand good examples.\n- **Avoid when** the task changes weekly, needs broad reasoning across many tools, or the gap is missing\n  knowledge; prompt a larger model or add retrieval instead.\n- **Measure** tool and argument accuracy, schema validity, task success in the full loop, latency and\n  fallback rate, before and after.\n- **Interviews:** explain data design (negatives, held-out split, template) before trainer settings.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the dataset from traces, runs LoRA or QLoRA with TRL and PEFT, and proves the adapter beats the prompted baseline on the agent eval before shipping it.","architect":"Decides which agent steps deserve a fine-tuned SLM, how adapters are versioned with their base model, and how fallback to a large model keeps correctness when an adapter regresses.","fde":"Scopes with the customer which step to specialise, what traces can legally be used for training, and what accuracy and cost targets the fine-tune must hit."},"check":{"q":"Your fine-tuned 3B router scores 96% on its test set but only 80% in production. List the likely\ncauses and how you would confirm each.","a":"First suspect test leakage: synthetic paraphrases of test cases may have landed in training, so\nre-split by session or user and re-score. Second, a chat template or tool-call parser mismatch between\ntraining and the serving engine; render a production request through the training template and diff it.\nThird, distribution shift: production has more ambiguous or \"no tool\" requests than the test set; sample\nand label production failures and compare category mix. Fourth, schema drift: a tool was renamed or gained\nan argument since the data was built. Fix by refreshing data from labelled production traces, adding\nnegatives, and gating on an eval set sampled from real traffic."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does QLoRA change compared with plain LoRA?","options":["It trains all weights of the model in 4-bit precision","It loads the frozen base model in 4-bit while training LoRA adapters in higher precision","It removes the need for a chat template","It replaces the adapter matrices with a quantized lookup table"],"answer":1,"explanation":"QLoRA quantizes the frozen base weights (typically NF4) to save memory, while the small LoRA adapters are still trained in higher precision, so large-ish bases fit on one GPU."},{"q":"A fine-tuned router calls a tool on almost every request, including greetings. What is the most likely data fix?","options":["Train for more epochs","Raise the LoRA rank to 128","Add \"no tool needed\" and clarification examples in realistic proportions","Remove the system prompt from training examples"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The model only learned that every example ends in a tool call. Including negatives and clarification cases teaches when not to call a tool; more epochs or rank would reinforce the bias."},{"q":"Why render training examples with the base model's own chat template?","options":["It makes training faster on GPUs","It is required for LoRA adapters to save correctly","Serving engines use that template and tool-call parser, so a mismatch breaks tool calls at inference","It reduces the number of trainable parameters"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The model learns the exact token layout it sees in training. If serving uses a different template or parser, outputs that looked right offline fail to parse as tool calls in the agent loop."}]},{"key":"slm-for-agents/distilling-a-large-model-agent-into-an-slm","sources":["Hugging Face TRL documentation (SFTTrainer, GKDTrainer)","OpenAI API documentation (function calling)","Hinton et al., 2015 — Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network"],"why":"- **Large models run the agent well but cost too much at volume.** A frontier model per step, many steps\n  per task, many tasks per day adds up quickly, and its latency compounds across the loop.\n- **SLMs lack training data for your tools.** There are no public traces of your internal APIs, and\n  hand-labelling multi-step trajectories is slow and expensive.\n- **The teacher can generate that data on demand.** It already follows your system prompt and tool\n  schemas, so its successful runs are demonstrations in exactly the right format.\n- **Filtering turns imitation into improvement.** Training only on verified successes removes much of the\n  teacher's failure noise, which is why a well-distilled student can approach the teacher on a narrow task.\n- **Check the terms.** Some providers' terms restrict using outputs to train competing models; confirm the\n  teacher's licence or terms before you start.","how":"### 1. Build the task distribution\n- **Sample inputs that match production:** real user requests (with consent), plus synthetic variants for\n  rare tools and edge cases. Coverage of the input space limits what the student can learn.\n- **Freeze the environment:** same tool schemas, same system prompt, tools backed by sandboxes or recorded\n  fixtures so runs are repeatable and safe.\n\n### 2. Run the teacher and log full trajectories\n- Record every step: messages, tool calls with arguments, tool results, final answer, and metadata (model,\n  prompt version, latency, token counts).\n- Run several samples per input at moderate temperature when tasks are hard, because more attempts give the\n  filter more successes to choose from (rejection sampling).\n\n### 3. Verify and filter\n| Check | Example |\n|---|---|\n| Programmatic success | the ticket was created, tests passed, the SQL returned the expected rows |\n| Schema validity | every tool call parses and matches its JSON Schema |\n| Efficiency | drop runs with loops, redundant calls or far more steps than the median |\n| Judge or human review | an LLM judge or reviewer grades answers that cannot be checked by code |\n| Safety | drop runs that touched forbidden tools or leaked data |\n\n### 4. Convert trajectories into training examples\n- **Whole trajectory as one conversation:** the student learns every assistant turn (tool calls and final\n  answer) with tool results as context; train loss on assistant turns only.\n- **Per-step examples:** split long runs so each example is \"context so far, next action\"; useful when the\n  student only takes over some steps.\n- **Strip teacher-only content:** hidden reasoning you cannot reproduce, provider-specific fields, or prompts\n  the student will not see at inference.\n\n### 5. Train the student, then close the gap\n1. Supervised fine-tuning (LoRA or full) on filtered trajectories; see fine-tuning-an-slm-for-an-agent-task.\n2. Evaluate the student in the real loop against the teacher on held-out tasks.\n3. Iterate on failures: run the teacher on inputs where the student fails, filter, add, retrain.\n4. Optional, open-weight teachers only: on-policy distillation where the student generates and the teacher\n   scores or supplies token distributions. Token-level matching usually needs teacher and student to share\n   a tokenizer, for example two sizes of one model family (TRL has a GKD trainer; check the current docs for\n   its API and location).","example":"### Collect and filter teacher trajectories\nThis runs a teacher through an OpenAI-compatible chat API with one tool, verifies the outcome, and writes\nonly successful runs as `{\"messages\": [...]}` lines for SFT. Point `base_url` at any compatible provider.\n\n```python\nimport json, os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()                       # set OPENAI_API_KEY, or base_url for another compatible provider\nTEACHER = os.environ[\"TEACHER_MODEL\"]   # read the current model id from the provider's docs\nTOOLS = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"get_order\", \"description\": \"Look up an order by id\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                   \"required\": [\"order_id\"]}}}]\nORDERS = {\"A17\": \"shipped\", \"B22\": \"refunded\"}          # sandboxed fixture, not production\n\ndef run_teacher(question, max_steps=4):\n    msgs = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are an order-support agent. Use tools.\"},\n            {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n    for _ in range(max_steps):\n        r = client.chat.completions.create(model=TEACHER, messages=msgs, tools=TOOLS)\n        m = r.choices[0].message\n        msgs.append(m.model_dump(exclude_none=True))\n        if not m.tool_calls:\n            return msgs\n        for c in m.tool_calls:\n            oid = json.loads(c.function.arguments).get(\"order_id\", \"\")\n            msgs.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": c.id, \"content\": ORDERS.get(oid, \"not found\")})\n    return None                                          # hit the step budget: treat as failure\n\ndef verified(msgs, expected):\n    return msgs is not None and expected in (msgs[-1].get(\"content\") or \"\").lower()\n\ncases = [(\"Where is order A17?\", \"shipped\"), (\"What happened to B22?\", \"refunded\")]\nwith open(\"distill.jsonl\", \"w\") as f:\n    for q, want in cases:\n        traj = run_teacher(q)\n        if verified(traj, want):\n            f.write(json.dumps({\"messages\": traj, \"tools\": TOOLS}) + \"\\n\")\n```\n\nFeed `distill.jsonl` to the SFT recipe in fine-tuning-an-slm-for-an-agent-task. Before training, check two\nformat details:\n- **Tool-call arguments:** the OpenAI API returns `arguments` as a JSON string, while many Hugging Face chat\n  templates expect a dict; convert with `json.loads` and inspect one rendered example.\n- **The `tools` column:** whether your trainer reads it and renders it into the chat template depends on its\n  version; check the current docs.\n\n### Worked scenario: distilling an order-support agent\nIllustrative numbers for a 3-tool support agent, teacher = large hosted model, student = 3B model.\n\n| Stage | Count or result |\n|---|---|\n| Task inputs (real + synthetic) | 5,000 |\n| Teacher runs (2 samples each) | 10,000 |\n| Passed verifier | 7,200 (72%) |\n| After dedup and efficiency filter | 4,100 trajectories |\n| Student task success, prompted only | ~55% |\n| Student after SFT on 4,100 | ~85% |\n| After one round on student failures (+900) | ~89% |\n| Teacher task success | ~92% |\n\n- **Cost shape:** teacher generation is a one-off cost per data round; inference savings recur on every\n  request, which is why distillation pays off on high-volume agents.\n- **Deployment:** the student handles all traffic; runs it cannot finish within the step budget escalate to\n  the teacher, and those runs become the next round's training data.","patterns":["**Use your tracing store as the data pipeline.** Log teacher runs with prompt version and tool schema version in LangSmith, Langfuse or OpenTelemetry spans, tag pass or fail from the verifier, and export only passing traces. Monitor pass rate per task type to spot coverage gaps.","**Prefer programmatic verifiers.** Check side effects in a sandbox (row created, test passed, API state) before falling back to an LLM judge, because judge errors become training labels. Spot-check a sample of judge-passed traces by hand each round.","**Distill step by step, not all at once.** Start with the highest-volume, easiest-to-verify step (routing or extraction), keep the teacher on planning, and expand the student's scope as evals allow.","**Run the failure loop.** Sample student failures or escalations from production, rerun the teacher on them, verify, add and retrain on a cadence. Track the escalation rate as the headline metric.","**Record licences and data lineage.** Store the teacher model, its terms, and the source of every input with the dataset version, so legal review and data-deletion requests can be answered."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** It is the usual way to get training data for an SLM agent step when you have\n  no labelled traces: the expensive model writes them, your verifier filters them, the cheap model learns.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Your agent framework runs the teacher; tracing (LangSmith, Langfuse,\n  OpenTelemetry) stores trajectories; TRL or a hosted fine-tuning API trains the student.\n- **What you control.** The task distribution, the success check that filters trajectories, how traces are\n  converted into training turns, and which steps the student takes over.\n- **What you can skip.** Logit-matching losses and temperature-softened targets from classic distillation;\n  with closed teacher APIs you only see text, so most agent distillation is sequence-level imitation.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **High-volume support agents.** A large model's resolved tickets train a small model that handles most\n  lookups and status questions.\n- **Text-to-SQL and analytics agents.** Execution-verified teacher queries train a student for a company's\n  own schema.\n- **Coding-agent sub-steps.** Test-verified edits or command choices from a large model train a small model\n  for fast, local steps.\n- **Privacy-driven migration.** Teams prototype with a hosted model, then distill into an open model they can\n  run on their own infrastructure.","mini_project":"**Distill a two-tool agent into a small model (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** collect verified teacher trajectories for a two-tool agent and show a small student improves\n  after training on them.\n- **Steps:** build a sandboxed agent with two fixture-backed tools; generate 300-500 varied requests; run a\n  large model as teacher, log trajectories, and keep those that pass a programmatic check; fine-tune a\n  0.5-3B model with LoRA; evaluate teacher, prompted student and distilled student on 100 held-out requests.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of verifier pass rate and task success for the three models, plus three\n  examples of failures you would target in the next data round."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Production-like task inputs\"] --> T[\"Teacher agent: large model\"]\n    T --> R[(\"Trajectory store\")]\n    R --> V{\"Verifier: success, schema, efficiency\"}\n    V -->|\"fail\"| X[\"Discard\"]\n    V -->|\"pass\"| C[\"Convert to chat training data\"]\n    C --> S[\"Fine-tune student SLM\"]\n    S --> E[\"Eval in agent loop vs teacher\"]\n    E -->|\"student fails\"| I\n    E -->|\"meets gate\"| D[\"Deploy with fallback to teacher\"]","caption":"Only verified teacher runs reach the student; student failures feed back into new teacher runs."},"interview":[{"q":"How is agent distillation different from classic knowledge distillation?","a":"- **Classic distillation** trains the student to match the teacher's softened output distribution (logits)\n  on each token, which needs access to the teacher's probabilities.\n- **Agent distillation** usually only has the teacher's text: it is supervised fine-tuning on filtered,\n  successful trajectories, including tool calls, so the verifier and data coverage matter most."},{"q":"How would you design the verifier for distilling a SQL-writing agent?","a":"- **Execute the query in a sandboxed copy** of the database and compare results with a reference or with\n  checks on expected properties.\n- **Reject** syntax errors, queries touching forbidden tables, and runs with excessive steps.\n- **Use an LLM judge only for the natural-language answer**, and spot-check its decisions by hand."},{"q":"Your student matches the teacher on simple requests but fails multi-step ones. What would you do?","a":"- **Inspect the data mix:** the filter may keep mostly short successful runs, so hard tasks are\n  under-represented.\n- **Sample more teacher attempts on hard inputs** to collect enough successes, consider per-step examples\n  for long runs, and keep escalation to the teacher for tasks over a step budget."},{"q":"When is distillation not worth it?","a":"- **Low traffic or volatile scope:** teacher runs, verifiers and retraining are a fixed cost that only pays\n  back with volume and a stable tool set.\n- **Unverifiable tasks:** without a reliable success signal you are imitating noise, so prompting a capable\n  model or a cascade is cheaper and safer."},{"q":"What metrics would you put on a dashboard for a distilled agent in production?","a":"- **Escalation rate to the teacher**, student task success and tool-call validity, by intent.\n- **Cost and latency per completed task** for student and teacher paths, and data-round versions, so a\n  regression can be traced to a specific dataset and model."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cheap inference vs upfront data cost.** Distillation trades a one-off bill for teacher runs and engineering for lower recurring cost and latency; it flips when traffic is too low to amortise it.","**Imitation vs ceiling.** The student rarely exceeds the teacher on the task, and it inherits the teacher's blind spots wherever the verifier cannot catch them.","**Whole trajectories vs per-step examples.** Whole runs teach multi-step coherence but need longer context and more data; per-step examples are simpler and fit hybrid deployments.","**Strict vs loose filtering.** A strict verifier yields cleaner but fewer and easier examples, which can bias the student away from hard cases; balance by sampling more teacher attempts on hard inputs."],"pitfalls":["**Training on unverified teacher output.** Symptom: the student confidently repeats the teacher's mistakes. Fix: add a programmatic or reviewed success check and drop failures.","**Environment drift between data and serving.** Symptom: the student calls tools that were renamed. Fix: version tool schemas with the dataset and regenerate when they change.","**Narrow input coverage.** Symptom: strong offline scores, poor results on rare intents. Fix: compare intent mix of training inputs with production and generate targeted inputs for gaps.","**Ignoring provider terms.** Symptom: legal blocks the launch late. Fix: confirm the teacher's terms allow training on outputs before generating data, or use an open-weight teacher."],"when":"- **Use when** a large-model agent already works, traffic is high enough that per-call cost or latency\n  matters, and you can verify success automatically for most tasks.\n- **Avoid when** the agent is still changing weekly, success cannot be checked, or volume is low enough that\n  paying for the large model is cheaper than a data pipeline.\n- **Measure** verifier pass rate, student vs teacher task success on held-out tasks, escalation rate in\n  production, and cost per completed task.\n- **Interviews:** stress verification and the failure loop, not the training loss.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the teacher harness, verifiers and trace export, converts trajectories into training data, and compares student and teacher on the same agent eval.","architect":"Decides which agent steps are distilled, how the student falls back to the teacher, and how dataset versions, tool schemas and model versions stay linked.","fde":"Confirms with the customer that teacher outputs and their data may be used for training, and sets cost and accuracy targets that justify the distillation project."},"check":{"q":"You distilled a large-model agent into a 3B model. Offline success is 88%, but in production the\nescalation rate to the teacher is 35%. What do you investigate and change?","a":"Compare the production input distribution with the training inputs, by intent and tool, because the\nmost likely cause is coverage: production contains request types the teacher never ran on. Check whether\ntool schemas or prompts changed since the data was built. Inspect escalated traces to see whether failures\nare format errors, wrong tools or long multi-step tasks beyond the student. Then run the teacher on a\nsample of escalated inputs, verify the runs, add them to training and retrain, repeating until the\nescalation rate falls. Also confirm the confidence or validation rule that triggers escalation is not too\nstrict."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do agent-distillation pipelines filter teacher trajectories before training?","options":["Filtering reduces the student's parameter count","Training only on verified successes keeps the teacher's mistakes out of the student","Unfiltered trajectories cannot be tokenized","Filtering is required to match the teacher's logits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The student imitates whatever it is trained on. A verifier that checks outcomes, schemas and efficiency removes failed or wasteful runs, so the student learns from the teacher's best behaviour."},{"q":"With a closed teacher available only through an API, what form of distillation is usually possible?","options":["Matching the teacher's per-token logits with a KL loss","Copying the teacher's weights into the student","Sequence-level imitation: fine-tuning the student on the teacher's generated trajectories","Pruning the teacher into a smaller model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"APIs return text, not full token distributions or weights, so the practical method is supervised fine-tuning on filtered teacher outputs. Logit-based methods need an open-weight teacher."},{"q":"After deployment, what is the most useful source of new distillation data?","options":["Random samples from a public web corpus","The student's own successful runs only","Inputs where the student failed or escalated, rerun through the teacher and verified","Repeating the original training set for more epochs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Failures and escalations show exactly where the student's coverage is weak. Running the teacher on those inputs and keeping verified successes targets the gap directly."}]},{"key":"slm-for-agents/slm-llm-cascades-and-routing","sources":["Ollama documentation (OpenAI compatibility)","vLLM documentation (OpenAI-compatible server)","LiteLLM documentation (Router, fallbacks)","Chen et al., 2023 — FrugalGPT"],"why":"- **Agent steps vary wildly in difficulty.** A tool-routing call and a multi-step recovery plan sit in the\n  same loop, but only the second needs a frontier model.\n- **One model for everything is a bad average.** Large-only overpays and adds latency per step; SLM-only\n  fails the hard tail and the whole task fails with it.\n- **Agents give you cheap checks.** Tool calls have JSON Schemas, many outputs can be validated by code,\n  and tool results reveal errors, so you can often tell when the small model got it wrong.\n- **What it buys.** If most calls stay on the SLM, cost per task falls roughly in proportion and latency\n  drops, while the escalated tail keeps large-model quality (actual savings depend on your escalation rate).","how":"### Three shapes\n| Shape | How it decides | Good for | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Static per-step assignment | config: router step on SLM, planner on LLM | stable agents with known step types | no extra calls |\n| Router (predict first) | classifier or rules pick a model before calling | when escalation after the fact is expensive | one router decision plus one model call |\n| Cascade (try, check, escalate) | SLM answers, a check accepts or escalates | when correctness is checkable | escalated calls pay for both models |\n\n### Escalation signals, strongest first\n1. **Hard validation:** the tool call fails JSON Schema, names an unknown tool, or misses required\n   arguments. Cheap and reliable.\n2. **Execution feedback:** the tool returns an error, the SQL fails, a test fails; escalate the retry.\n3. **Verifier or judge:** a separate check (rules, a small classifier, an LLM judge) scores the output.\n4. **Model confidence:** token log-probabilities or self-reported confidence. Cheap but often poorly\n   calibrated for small models; calibrate on your eval set before trusting a threshold.\n5. **Budgets:** the SLM exceeded a step or retry budget in the loop; hand the task to the large model.\n\n### Where to cascade in an agent loop\n- **Per call:** each model call cascades independently. Simple, but a long trajectory built by the SLM may\n  confuse the large model when it takes over mid-run.\n- **Per task:** the SLM runs the whole task; on failure, rerun from the start with the large model. Costs\n  more on failure but keeps trajectories coherent.\n- **Per role:** the large model plans, SLMs execute well-specified sub-steps (extraction, tool calls).\n\n### Setting the threshold\n- Build an eval set with labels, run both tiers, and plot quality and cost against the escalation\n  threshold. Pick the point that meets the quality target at the lowest cost.\n- Re-tune when either model or the traffic mix changes, because the curve moves.\n- **Know the break-even.** With escalation rate `e`, a cascade costs about `c_small + e × c_large` per call\n  versus `c_large` for large-only, so it saves money while `e < 1 − c_small / c_large`:\n\n```\nc_small = 1, c_large = 20   ->  break-even at e = 95%  (20% escalation costs 5 units, a quarter of 20)\nc_small = 1, c_large = 2    ->  break-even at e = 50%  (small tier barely cheaper: cascade rarely pays)\n```\n\n- **Count the whole small tier.** `c_small` includes any verifier or judge call, retries, and the real cost\n  of a dedicated GPU at your utilisation, which is how cascades end up costing more than large-only.","example":"### A validate-then-escalate cascade for one tool-calling step\nThe SLM runs locally through Ollama's OpenAI-compatible endpoint; the large model through any compatible\nprovider. Pydantic validation is the escalation signal.\n\n```python\nimport json, os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError\n\nsmall = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\")   # Ollama OpenAI-compatible API\nlarge = OpenAI()                                                       # hosted, uses OPENAI_API_KEY\nSMALL_MODEL, LARGE_MODEL = \"qwen2.5:3b\", os.environ[\"LARGE_MODEL\"]     # check current model ids\n\nclass Route(BaseModel):\n    tool: str\n    order_id: str | None = None\n\nTOOLS = {\"get_order\", \"refund_order\", \"handoff_human\"}\nPROMPT = (\"Pick one tool from get_order, refund_order, handoff_human for the request. \"\n          'Reply with JSON only: {\"tool\": ..., \"order_id\": ...}')\n\ndef ask(client, model, text):\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=model, temperature=0,\n        response_format={\"type\": \"json_object\"},\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": PROMPT}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": text}])\n    return r.choices[0].message.content\n\ndef route(text):\n    try:\n        out = Route.model_validate(json.loads(ask(small, SMALL_MODEL, text)))\n        if out.tool in TOOLS and (out.tool == \"handoff_human\" or out.order_id):\n            return out, \"slm\"\n    except (json.JSONDecodeError, ValidationError):\n        pass                                        # invalid output is the escalation signal\n    return Route.model_validate(json.loads(ask(large, LARGE_MODEL, text))), \"llm\"\n\nfor q in [\"Where is order A17?\", \"I want my money back for B22\", \"Your app is awful, get me a person\"]:\n    print(route(q))\n```\n\nLog the tier and the reason for every escalation; those rows are your best data for fine-tuning the SLM\nlater.\n\n### Worked scenario: choosing the threshold for an agent's routing step\nIllustrative eval on 1,000 labelled requests, cost in relative units (SLM call = 1, large call = 20,\nverifier cost left out for simplicity).\n\n| Policy | Escalation rate | Routing accuracy | Cost per 1,000 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Large model only | 100% | 96% | 20,000 |\n| SLM only | 0% | 84% | 1,000 |\n| Cascade on schema failure only | 6% | 88% | 2,200 |\n| Cascade on schema failure or verifier reject | 18% | 95% | 4,600 |\n| Same, plus escalate on any tool error | 21% | 96% | 5,200 |\n\n- **Read the table:** schema checks alone miss wrong-but-valid tool choices; a verifier catches most of them\n  at about a quarter of the large-only cost.\n- **Decision:** ship the last row, alert if escalation rate drifts above 30%, and use escalated cases to\n  fine-tune the SLM so the rate falls over time.","patterns":["**Put routing in one place.** Implement tiers in a gateway or a LiteLLM Router with fallbacks, not in every agent, so model swaps and thresholds change in config. Monitor requests and spend per tier.","**Escalate on validation first.** Use JSON Schema or Pydantic validation and unknown-tool checks as the default signal because they are free and precise; add judges only where valid-but-wrong outputs matter.","**Track escalation rate as a health metric.** Alert on sudden rises, which usually mean a prompt change, a new intent or an SLM serving problem, and break it down by step and reason.","**Feed escalations into training.** Store the input, the SLM output and the large-model output for each escalation, label a sample, and fine-tune or distill the SLM on it; the cascade becomes cheaper each round.","**Bound latency for the worst case.** A cascaded call pays SLM latency plus large-model latency, so set timeouts on the SLM, run the SLM locally or nearby, and consider racing both tiers for latency-critical steps. Monitor p95 latency for escalated calls separately."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Most agent calls are easy (route, extract, format, summarise a tool\n  result) and a few are hard (plan, recover from an error). Paying large-model prices for every step wastes\n  most of the budget.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A thin wrapper around your model client, a gateway or LiteLLM router,\n  per-step model settings in your agent framework, and Ollama or vLLM serving the SLM behind an\n  OpenAI-compatible API.\n- **What you control.** Which steps go to which tier, the escalation signal (validation, confidence,\n  verifier, step budget), and the cost, latency and quality targets.\n- **What you can skip.** Learned-router research details; system-design/model-routing-cascading covers the\n  general design. Here the focus is SLM-first agent loops.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Support and operations agents.** An SLM handles routing and lookups; refunds, complaints and ambiguous\n  requests escalate to a large model.\n- **Coding agents.** Small models do file search, summaries and simple edits; planning and failed-test\n  recovery go to a large model.\n- **Document-processing pipelines.** Extraction runs on an SLM with schema checks; documents that fail\n  validation are re-extracted by a large model.\n- **Cost-capped multi-tenant platforms.** A gateway routes per tenant tier and per step to keep spend within\n  budget.","mini_project":"**Build and tune a two-tier cascade (2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** cut the cost of an agent's routing step while keeping accuracy within one point of a large-only\n  baseline.\n- **Steps:** serve a small model with Ollama; write a cascade wrapper with Pydantic validation and an\n  optional verifier; label 200 routing requests; run large-only, SLM-only and two cascade policies; record\n  escalation rate, accuracy, and relative cost per policy.\n- **Done when:** you have a cost-quality table, a chosen policy with its threshold, and a log of escalated\n  cases ready to use as fine-tuning data."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Agent step request\"] --> S[\"SLM call\"]\n    S --> V{\"Schema valid and check passes?\"}\n    V -->|\"yes\"| A[\"Use SLM result\"]\n    V -->|\"no\"| L[\"Large model call\"]\n    L --> A2[\"Use LLM result\"]\n    A --> T[\"Execute tool\"]\n    A2 --> T\n    T -->|\"tool error\"| L\n    A2 -.->|\"log for retraining\"| D[(\"Escalation dataset\")]","caption":"The SLM answers first; only calls that fail a check pay for the large model."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a router and a cascade, and when would you pick each?","a":"- **A router predicts the right model before calling**, so each request pays for one model; it fits when\n  wrong-model calls are expensive or outputs are hard to check.\n- **A cascade tries the small model and escalates on a failed check**, which fits agents because tool calls\n  and structured outputs are easy to validate."},{"q":"Which escalation signals would you use for an SLM that calls tools, and why?","a":"- **Schema and tool-name validation first:** free, precise, and catches the most common SLM failure.\n- **Execution feedback next:** tool errors or failed tests trigger a retry on the large model.\n- **Verifiers or calibrated confidence** only where valid-but-wrong outputs matter, after measuring them on\n  labelled data."},{"q":"How would you choose the escalation threshold?","a":"- **Run both tiers on a labelled eval set** of real agent calls and compute task success and cost at each\n  threshold.\n- **Pick the cheapest point that meets the quality target**, set an alert on escalation rate, and rerun the\n  sweep when models, prompts or traffic change."},{"q":"When would you not use an SLM-first cascade?","a":"- **When most steps are hard:** high escalation means paying twice; use a large model or route hard step\n  types directly.\n- **When outputs are not checkable or tail latency is tight:** without a reliable signal the cascade is\n  guessing, and escalated calls add the SLM's latency to the large model's."},{"q":"How do you make a cascade cheaper over time?","a":"- **Log every escalation** with input, SLM output and large-model output.\n- **Label a sample, fine-tune or distill the SLM on it**, re-evaluate, and watch the escalation rate fall\n  release by release."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cost vs tail latency.** Cascades save money on the common case but make escalated calls slower than a direct large-model call; routers avoid that at the price of an up-front prediction that can be wrong.","**Simple checks vs coverage.** Schema validation is cheap but misses valid-but-wrong answers; verifiers catch more but add their own cost, latency and errors.","**Per-call vs per-task escalation.** Per-call is cheaper but mixes two models in one trajectory; per-task is coherent but wastes the SLM's partial work when it fails.","**More tiers vs operability.** Three or more tiers can save more, but each adds models to evaluate, serve and monitor; the gain flips when traffic is low."],"pitfalls":["**Trusting raw confidence.** Symptom: the SLM is confidently wrong and never escalates. Fix: calibrate confidence against labelled data, or use validation and verifiers instead.","**No check for valid-but-wrong outputs.** Symptom: good escalation stats, bad task success. Fix: measure end-to-end task success, not just schema validity, and add a verifier where wrong tools are costly.","**Hidden double cost.** Symptom: the cascade costs more than large-only. Fix: recompute the break-even with the true small-tier cost (verifier calls, retries, idle GPU); if escalation rate is high, route those request types straight to the large model and fine-tune the SLM before retrying.","**Stale thresholds.** Symptom: quality drops after a model upgrade. Fix: rerun the threshold eval on every model, prompt or traffic-mix change."],"when":"- **Use when** most agent calls are easy and checkable, traffic is high enough that cost or latency\n  matters, and you have an eval set to tune escalation.\n- **Avoid when** nearly every step is hard (escalation rate would be high), outputs cannot be checked, or\n  the added tail latency breaks a user-facing budget.\n- **Measure** escalation rate by step and reason, end-to-end task success, cost per task and p95 latency\n  per tier.\n- **Interviews:** name the escalation signal and how you calibrated it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the cascade wrapper with schema validation and escalation logging, and tunes the threshold on a labelled eval set.","architect":"Chooses static assignment, router or cascade per agent step, and owns the gateway, budgets and escalation metrics across agents.","fde":"Shows a customer the cost-quality curve from their own data and agrees the escalation threshold that meets their accuracy target within budget."},"check":{"q":"Your SLM-first cascade for an agent's tool-selection step escalates 45% of calls and costs more than\nusing the large model alone. What happened, and what do you do?","a":"At 45% escalation, nearly half the calls pay for both models, so the cascade adds cost and latency.\nIt only costs more than large-only when the small tier is not much cheaper per call (break-even is\n1 − small cost / large cost), so first recount the small tier: verifier or judge calls, retries, or a\ndedicated GPU at low utilisation. Break escalations down by reason and request type: if a few intents dominate, route them straight to the\nlarge model with a router or rule. Check whether the escalation signal is too strict or badly calibrated,\nfor example a confidence threshold set without data. Check for an SLM serving or prompt problem causing\nschema failures. Then fine-tune or distill the SLM on the logged escalations so it handles those cases, and\nrerun the cost-quality curve to pick a new threshold."},"quiz":[{"q":"In an SLM-first cascade for a tool-calling agent, what is usually the cheapest reliable escalation signal?","options":["The SLM's self-reported confidence score","Failing JSON Schema validation or naming an unknown tool","A second call to the large model to compare answers","The length of the SLM's output"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Schema and tool-name checks cost nothing extra and precisely catch the most common SLM failures. Self-reported confidence is often poorly calibrated, and comparing with the large model removes the savings."},{"q":"Why can a cascade end up costing more than using only the large model?","options":["SLMs use more tokens per request than large models","Escalated calls pay for both models, so a high escalation rate erases the savings","Cascades disable prompt caching on the large model","Validation code is more expensive than model calls"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every escalated request runs the small model and then the large one. If too many requests escalate, total cost and latency exceed a direct large-model call."},{"q":"What is a drawback of escalating per call in the middle of an agent trajectory?","options":["The large model cannot call tools","Per-call escalation requires a learned router","The large model inherits a trajectory partly built by the SLM, which may contain its mistakes","It prevents logging escalations for retraining"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mid-run handoffs keep earlier SLM steps in context, including any wrong tool calls. Per-task escalation restarts cleanly at higher cost; teams choose based on how coherent trajectories must be."}]},{"key":"slm-for-agents/on-device-and-edge-agents-with-slms","sources":["llama.cpp documentation (GGUF, grammars)","llama-cpp-python documentation (chat completion, JSON schema mode)","Apple MLX documentation","ONNX Runtime documentation (generate API)","PyTorch ExecuTorch documentation"],"why":"- **Data cannot always leave the device.** Health notes, personal messages, regulated industrial data, or\n  customer policy rule out sending prompts to a cloud API.\n- **Connectivity is not guaranteed.** Field workers, vehicles and remote sites need an agent that still\n  works with no network.\n- **Round trips add latency.** A local SLM avoids network time for short, frequent steps like intent\n  detection, form filling or command parsing.\n- **Cloud cost scales with users.** On-device inference costs nothing per call once shipped, which matters\n  for consumer apps with millions of users.\n- **The catch:** a phone may give an app only a few GB of memory, sustained inference drains battery and\n  heats the device, and small models are weaker, so the agent must be designed around those limits.","how":"### 1. Fit the model to the device\n```\nweight_memory ≈ parameters × bits_per_weight / 8\ne.g. 3B × 4 bits / 8 ≈ 1.5 GB   (plus KV cache, runtime and app memory)\n```\n| Device class | Typical model size (4-bit) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Recent phone | ~0.5-3B | memory per app and thermals are the limits |\n| Laptop with 16 GB+ | ~3-8B | GPU or Apple unified memory helps a lot |\n| Edge box with a small GPU | ~3-14B | can serve several local users |\n| Browser (WebGPU) | ~0.5-3B | first load downloads the weights; cache them |\n\nRough orders of magnitude; check each runtime's docs and measure on the target device. Practical \"4-bit\"\nfiles run a little larger than the formula: GGUF types such as Q4_K_M keep some tensors at higher precision,\nso a 3B file is closer to 2 GB than 1.5 GB.\n\n### 2. Pick a runtime for the platform\n| Runtime | Where it fits | Model format |\n|---|---|---|\n| llama.cpp and bindings (llama-cpp-python, others) | desktop, server, mobile builds, Raspberry Pi class | GGUF |\n| Ollama | desktops and edge boxes, simple local API | GGUF-based |\n| MLX (mlx-lm) | Apple silicon Macs | MLX weights |\n| ONNX Runtime (generate API) | Windows, Android, cross-platform apps | ONNX |\n| ExecuTorch | iOS, Android, embedded, from PyTorch | exported PyTorch programs |\n| WebGPU runtimes (for example WebLLM) | in-browser agents | runtime-specific builds |\n\nPlatform vendors also expose built-in on-device models through OS APIs; capabilities and availability\nchange often, so check the current platform docs.\n\n### 3. Design the agent for a small model\n- **Few, narrow tools.** Expose 3-8 local tools with short schemas; every schema token costs context and\n  latency on a slow device.\n- **Constrain the output.** Use grammar or JSON-schema-constrained decoding (llama.cpp grammars, JSON\n  schema mode in llama-cpp-python) so tool calls always parse.\n- **Short context.** Keep context to a few thousand tokens, summarise or drop old turns, because KV cache\n  memory and prefill time grow with length.\n- **Specialise.** A LoRA fine-tune for the device's task often matters more than a bigger base model.\n\n### 4. Plan the cloud boundary\n- **Local-first with optional escalation:** hard requests go to a cloud model only with consent and\n  connectivity (see slm-llm-cascades-and-routing).\n- **Redact before escalation:** strip personal data on the device first if policy allows escalation at all.\n- **Sync later:** queue actions taken offline and reconcile with the server when the network returns.","example":"### A local tool-calling step with llama-cpp-python and a JSON schema\nRuns fully offline with a downloaded GGUF file. JSON schema mode constrains decoding, so the tool call\nalways parses even from a small model.\n\n```python\n# pip install llama-cpp-python   (build options for Metal or CUDA: see its docs)\nimport json\nfrom llama_cpp import Llama\n\nllm = Llama(model_path=\"models/small-instruct-q4_k_m.gguf\",   # any small instruct GGUF you downloaded\n            n_ctx=4096, n_gpu_layers=-1, verbose=False)        # -1 offloads all layers if a GPU exists\n\nSCHEMA = {\"type\": \"object\",\n          \"properties\": {\"tool\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"set_reminder\", \"search_notes\", \"none\"]},\n                         \"query\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                         \"time\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n          \"required\": [\"tool\"]}\n\ndef local_step(text):\n    r = llm.create_chat_completion(\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Pick a tool for the request. Reply in JSON.\"},\n                  {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": text}],\n        response_format={\"type\": \"json_object\", \"schema\": SCHEMA}, temperature=0)\n    return json.loads(r[\"choices\"][0][\"message\"][\"content\"])\n\nNOTES = [\"Gate code for site B is in the blue binder\", \"Pump 3 serviced in March\"]\ncall = local_step(\"When was pump 3 serviced?\")\nif call[\"tool\"] == \"search_notes\":\n    hits = [n for n in NOTES if any(w in n.lower() for w in call.get(\"query\", \"\").lower().split())]\n    print(hits)\nelse:\n    print(call)\n```\n\nOn a laptop this runs on CPU or Metal or CUDA; for phones, use a mobile runtime from the table with the\nsame design: small model, constrained output, local tools.\n\n### Worked scenario: a field-maintenance agent on a rugged tablet\nRequirements: works offline, keeps site data on the device, answers within about 2 seconds. Illustrative\nnumbers.\n\n| Decision | Choice | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Model | ~3B instruct, 4-bit GGUF, LoRA-tuned on maintenance requests | ~2 GB fits beside the app |\n| Context | 2k tokens, last 4 turns plus retrieved notes | keeps prefill short on a mobile chip |\n| Tools | search_manuals, log_fault, read_sensor, none | 4 short schemas, JSON-constrained |\n| Retrieval | small on-device embedding model plus local vector index | manuals searchable offline |\n| Cloud | escalate only when online and the technician taps \"ask expert\" | privacy and cost |\n| Sync | fault logs queued and uploaded when connected | offline-first |\n\n- **Measured on device (illustrative):** first token in well under a second for short prompts, a few tokens\n  per second to tens of tokens per second depending on chip, and acceptable battery drain for a shift\n  because calls are short.\n- **Biggest win:** constraining output and cutting tool schemas fixed most failures, more than switching to a\n  larger model.","patterns":["**Test on the slowest supported device.** Benchmark time to first token, tokens per second, peak memory and battery drain on the oldest target hardware, because a model that is fine on a developer laptop may be killed by the OS on a phone. Gate releases on those numbers.","**Download models separately from the app.** Ship the app small, fetch the model on first run over Wi-Fi, verify a checksum, and version models so you can roll back. Monitor download failures and storage errors.","**Always constrain tool calls.** Use grammars or JSON-schema decoding in the runtime and still validate in code, because a malformed call on a device cannot be retried against a stronger model when offline.","**Treat local tools as privileged.** On-device agents can reach files, contacts or sensors; give each tool least privilege and require user confirmation for destructive actions (see agents/sandboxing).","**Collect evals, not data.** Where users consent, report anonymous outcome signals (tool-call parse rate, escalation taps, latency) instead of raw prompts, and use them to choose what to fine-tune next."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Some agents must work offline, keep data on the device, or respond in\n  well under a second: field apps, assistants inside desktop software, kiosks, vehicles, factory gateways.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** llama.cpp (GGUF) and its bindings, Ollama on desktops, MLX on Apple\n  silicon, ONNX Runtime and ExecuTorch on mobile and embedded, WebGPU runtimes in the browser, and OS-level\n  on-device models where the platform provides one.\n- **What you control.** Model size and quantization, context length, the tool set exposed on the device,\n  constrained decoding for tool calls, and when to hand off to a cloud model.\n- **What you can skip.** Kernel-level optimisation and NPU compiler details; pick a runtime that supports\n  your target hardware and measure on real devices.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Field and industrial assistants.** Offline agents on tablets or gateways that search manuals, read\n  sensors and log faults at remote sites.\n- **Private personal assistants.** Note, email or health assistants that summarise and act on personal data\n  without sending it off the phone or laptop.\n- **In-app copilots.** Desktop software that parses natural-language commands into local actions with no\n  per-user API bill.\n- **Vehicles, kiosks and retail devices.** Voice or text agents that must answer instantly with unreliable\n  connectivity.","mini_project":"**Build an offline note agent on your laptop (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a local agent that answers questions over your notes and creates reminders with no network.\n- **Steps:** download a small instruct GGUF model; use llama-cpp-python with a JSON schema for three tools\n  (search_notes, set_reminder, none); implement tools over a local folder and a JSON file; disconnect from the\n  network; run 30 test requests and record tool-call accuracy, time to first token and peak memory; try a\n  second quantization level and compare.\n- **Done when:** the agent works offline, every tool call parses, and you have a table comparing two\n  quantization levels on accuracy, latency and memory."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"User request on device\"] --> M[\"Local SLM, quantized\"]\n    M -->|\"constrained tool call\"| T[\"Local tools: files, sensors, app APIs\"]\n    T --> M\n    M --> A[\"Answer or action\"]\n    M -->|\"too hard and policy allows\"| R[\"Redact on device\"]\n    R --> C[\"Cloud model\"]\n    C --> A\n    A -.->|\"offline actions queued\"| Q[(\"Sync queue\")]","caption":"The device handles the loop locally; the cloud is an optional, policy-gated fallback."},"interview":[{"q":"How do you decide which model size fits an on-device agent?","a":"- **Start from memory:** parameters times bits per weight over 8 gives weight size, then add KV cache for\n  your context length, runtime overhead and the app itself, and compare with what the OS allows the app.\n- **Then measure** time to first token, tokens per second and battery on the slowest target device, and pick\n  the largest model that meets the latency and memory budget."},{"q":"Why is constrained decoding especially important for on-device agents?","a":"- **Small models produce malformed tool calls more often**, and offline there is no stronger model to retry\n  against.\n- **Grammar or JSON-schema decoding** in the runtime guarantees parseable output, and code validation still\n  checks argument semantics."},{"q":"How would you design the boundary between an on-device agent and a cloud model?","a":"- **Local by default**, with escalation only when the request needs it, the user or policy allows it, and\n  the device is online.\n- **Redact on the device** before any cloud call, log escalations, and make the behaviour visible to users so\n  privacy guarantees are testable."},{"q":"When would you not put an agent on the device?","a":"- **When the task needs broad reasoning, long contexts or many tools**, because small quantized models\n  degrade fast there.\n- **When target hardware is too varied or old**, since device testing and per-platform runtimes may cost more\n  than a cloud deployment with good privacy controls."},{"q":"What would you monitor for an on-device agent you cannot log prompts from?","a":"- **Consented, anonymous outcome metrics:** tool-call parse rate, escalation or \"ask expert\" taps, task\n  completion signals, latency percentiles, crashes and out-of-memory kills by device model.\n- **Use them to target fine-tuning** and to choose minimum supported hardware."}],"tradeoffs":["**Privacy and offline vs capability.** Keeping everything local rules out frontier-model quality; the balance flips when a task needs broad reasoning and data policy allows a cloud call.","**Zero per-call cost vs engineering cost.** No inference bill, but you maintain model packaging, runtimes per platform and device testing across hardware generations.","**Smaller model vs better quantization.** A 4-bit 3B model may beat an 8-bit 1B in the same memory, or not; measure on your task rather than assuming.","**Latency vs battery.** Running the GPU or NPU flat out gives fast answers but drains battery and throttles under heat; short prompts and few calls per task keep both acceptable."],"pitfalls":["**Benchmarking only on a desktop.** Symptom: crashes or 10-second responses on real phones. Fix: test on target devices with realistic background apps and thermal state.","**Large tool schemas and long history.** Symptom: slow first token and context overflow. Fix: fewer, shorter tools and aggressive context trimming.","**Unconstrained output from a small model.** Symptom: occasional malformed tool calls that break the loop offline. Fix: grammar or schema-constrained decoding plus code validation.","**Silent cloud fallback.** Symptom: privacy review finds data leaving the device. Fix: make escalation explicit, consented, redacted and logged."],"when":"- **Use when** data must stay on the device, the agent must work offline, per-user cloud cost is\n  prohibitive, or local latency matters, and the task is narrow enough for a small model.\n- **Avoid when** the agent needs broad reasoning, many tools or long contexts, or target devices are too\n  constrained; use a cloud model or a hybrid design.\n- **Measure** task success, tool-call parse rate, time to first token, tokens per second, peak memory and\n  battery per task on target devices.\n- **Interviews:** connect memory arithmetic, runtime choice and the cloud boundary.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Packages a quantized model for the target runtime, builds constrained local tool calls, and benchmarks latency, memory and battery on real devices.","architect":"Defines the device-cloud boundary, model update and rollback strategy, and the privacy rules for when anything may leave the device.","fde":"Captures the customer's hardware, connectivity and data-residency constraints and turns them into a model-size and runtime choice they can sign off."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants an assistant on their field technicians' mid-range Android phones that works\noffline and never sends site data off the device. Sketch your design and the main risks.","a":"Use a roughly 1-3B instruct model quantized to 4-bit, run with an Android-capable runtime such as\nllama.cpp, ONNX Runtime or ExecuTorch, downloaded on first launch with checksum and version. Keep the tool\nset small (search manuals, log fault, read checklist) with JSON-schema-constrained calls and code\nvalidation. Use an on-device embedding model and local index for manuals, a short context window, and a\nLoRA fine-tune on technician requests. Queue logs for sync. Main risks: memory limits and thermal\nthrottling on older phones, weaker reasoning on unusual faults, and model updates; mitigate with on-device\nbenchmarks, an explicit \"contact expert\" path, and staged rollouts."},"quiz":[{"q":"Roughly how much memory do the weights of a 3B-parameter model take at 4-bit quantization?","options":["About 0.4 GB","About 1.5 GB","About 6 GB","About 12 GB"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Weight memory is parameters times bits per weight divided by 8: 3B × 4 / 8 ≈ 1.5 GB. KV cache, runtime and app memory come on top, which is why phones usually run models of a few billion parameters at most."},{"q":"Why should an on-device agent use grammar or JSON-schema constrained decoding for tool calls?","options":["It makes the model larger and more accurate","Small models misformat calls more often and offline there is no stronger model to fall back to","It is required by every mobile operating system","It removes the need to validate arguments in code"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Constraining decoding guarantees the output parses, which is the most common small-model failure. Arguments can still be semantically wrong, so code validation remains necessary."},{"q":"What is the safest default for cloud escalation in a privacy-sensitive on-device agent?","options":["Escalate automatically whenever the device is online","Never allow any network access for the app","Escalate only when policy and the user allow it, after on-device redaction, and log it","Send all requests to the cloud and cache answers locally"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Explicit, consented, redacted escalation keeps privacy guarantees testable while still offering a path for hard requests. Silent fallback is the classic privacy failure."}]},{"key":"slm-for-agents/evaluating-slms-on-your-agent-tasks","sources":["Ollama documentation (OpenAI compatibility)","vLLM documentation (OpenAI-compatible server)","EleutherAI lm-evaluation-harness documentation","Berkeley Function Calling Leaderboard documentation"],"why":"- **General benchmarks measure the wrong thing.** Knowledge and reasoning scores say little about following\n  your tool schemas, staying in format for many steps, or refusing when a tool is missing.\n- **SLM behaviour is fragile.** Quantization level, chat template, sampling settings and prompt wording can\n  each move small-model accuracy by several points, so you must test the exact configuration you will\n  serve.\n- **Agent errors compound.** A per-step success rate of 95% over 10 steps leaves about 60% of tasks fully\n  correct (0.95^10 ≈ 0.6, assuming independent steps), so step-level and task-level metrics both matter.\n- **Model families change monthly.** New Llama, Qwen, Phi, Gemma and Mistral releases arrive often; a\n  reusable eval lets you re-test a new model in an hour instead of guessing from a model card.","how":"### 1. Build the eval set from your agent\n| Slice | Example cases | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tool selection | each tool, plus \"no tool\" | catches over-calling and wrong tools |\n| Argument filling | required, optional, typed, enum arguments | catches invented or missing arguments |\n| Structured output | extraction into your Pydantic or JSON schema | format compliance |\n| Multi-step tasks | 3-10 step runs in a sandbox | compounding errors and loops |\n| Robustness | paraphrases, typos, long context, distracting tool results | small-model fragility |\n| Safety | prompt injection in tool results, forbidden actions | agents act, so failures have effects |\n\n- **Source cases** from production traces, SME-written cases and synthetic variants; keep 100-300 cases per\n  slice for a first pass (illustrative; enough to see differences of a few points).\n- **Freeze it** as a versioned golden set and never train on it.\n\n### 2. Define metrics per level\n- **Call level:** parse rate (valid JSON or tool call), schema validity, tool-name accuracy, argument exact\n  match or field-level match.\n- **Task level:** task success checked by code in a sandbox (state changed correctly), steps taken, loops,\n  and a judge score only where code cannot check.\n- **Operational:** time to first token, tokens per second, p95 latency per step, memory, and cost per\n  successful task on your hardware.\n\n### 3. Run it like an experiment\n1. **Fix everything except the model:** same prompts, tool schemas, chat template handling, decoding\n   settings and hardware.\n2. **Test the served configuration:** the exact quantization (for example Q4 vs Q8 GGUF, AWQ) and engine.\n3. **Repeat runs** with sampling on (or several seeds), because small models are less stable; report mean and\n   spread, or pass@k and pass^k for tasks (see evals/pass-k).\n4. **Compare against a baseline:** the current production model or a large model, on the same set.\n5. **Read failures**, not only scores: categorise them (wrong tool, bad argument, format, loop) to decide\n   between prompting, constrained decoding, fine-tuning or a cascade.\n\n### 4. Gate releases and monitor\n- Set thresholds per slice (for example schema validity at least 99%, task success within 2 points of the\n  baseline) and block a model swap that misses any of them.\n- Sample production traces into the eval set regularly so it tracks real traffic.","example":"### A minimal tool-call eval across local models\nCompares models served by Ollama (or any OpenAI-compatible server) on tool name, argument match and\nlatency. Each case lists the expected tool and arguments.\n\n```python\nimport json, statistics, time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\")\nMODELS = [\"qwen2.5:3b\", \"llama3.2:3b\"]       # whatever candidates you pulled; check current tags\nTOOLS = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"get_weather\", \"description\": \"Weather for a city\",\n          \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"city\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"city\"]}}},\n         {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"convert_currency\", \"description\": \"Convert money\",\n          \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"amount\": {\"type\": \"number\"},\n          \"to\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"amount\", \"to\"]}}}]\nCASES = [(\"Is it raining in Lisbon?\", \"get_weather\", {\"city\": \"Lisbon\"}),\n         (\"Turn 40 dollars into euros\", \"convert_currency\", {\"amount\": 40, \"to\": \"EUR\"}),\n         (\"Tell me a joke\", None, None)]\n\ndef run(model, text):\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=model, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": text}],\n                                       tools=TOOLS, temperature=0)\n    calls = r.choices[0].message.tool_calls or []\n    if not calls:\n        return None, None, time.perf_counter() - t\n    try:\n        args = json.loads(calls[0].function.arguments)\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        args = \"INVALID\"\n    return calls[0].function.name, args, time.perf_counter() - t\n\nfor m in MODELS:\n    ok, lat = 0, []\n    for text, want_tool, want_args in CASES:\n        tool, args, s = run(m, text)\n        lat.append(s)\n        ok += tool == want_tool and (want_args is None or args == want_args)\n    print(f\"{m}: exact {ok}/{len(CASES)}, median latency {statistics.median(lat):.2f}s\")\n```\n\nGrow `CASES` to hundreds, add a schema check, repeat with sampling on, and add a multi-step sandbox suite\nbefore trusting the ranking.\n\n### Worked scenario: picking an SLM for an extraction-and-routing agent\nIllustrative scorecard on 600 cases, same prompts and hardware, three runs each.\n\n| Candidate | Parse rate | Tool accuracy | Task success | p95 step latency | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Large hosted model (baseline) | 100% | 96% | 93% | 1.8 s | reference |\n| 7B, 4-bit | 99.5% | 93% | 89% | 0.9 s | strong candidate |\n| 3B, 8-bit | 98% | 90% | 84% | 0.5 s | good with a cascade |\n| 3B, 4-bit | 95% | 86% | 77% | 0.4 s | quantization hurt; reject |\n| 1B, 8-bit | 88% | 74% | 61% | 0.3 s | too weak for this task |\n\n- **Insight:** the 3B model lost 7 task-success points going from 8-bit to 4-bit, so the quantized model,\n  not the family, was the real choice to evaluate.\n- **Read it against the gate:** no SLM alone is within 2 points of the 93% baseline, so none passes the\n  example gate in section 4 by itself; the 7B 4-bit model comes closest at 89%.\n- **Decision:** no SLM ships alone. Put the 3B 8-bit model (or the 7B 4-bit if hardware allows) behind a\n  cascade that escalates on schema failure or verifier reject, score the cascade as a whole on the same 600\n  cases against the gate, and ship only if it passes. Re-run this scorecard when a new model family appears.","patterns":["**Keep the harness model-agnostic.** Call every candidate through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint (Ollama, vLLM, llama.cpp server, hosted APIs) so a new model is a config line. Store results per model, quantization and prompt version.","**Run evals in CI on any model or prompt change.** Treat a model swap like a code change: the eval suite runs, and a slice below its threshold blocks the merge. Track trends per slice over releases.","**Refresh the golden set from production.** Sample traces weekly, especially escalations and failures, have them labelled, and add them as new cases so the eval tracks real traffic. Version the dataset.","**Measure cost per successful task.** Combine success rate with tokens, latency and hardware cost, because a cheaper model that fails more can cost more per completed task once retries and escalations count.","**Shadow before switching.** Run the candidate SLM on live traffic in shadow mode, compare its outputs with production, and only then canary it. Monitor parse rate and task success by model version."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Small models vary far more than large ones across tasks, quantization\n  levels and prompt formats. A model that tops a general benchmark can still misname your tools or break\n  your JSON.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** An eval harness (your own pytest-style script, or tools covered in\n  evals/tooling), OpenAI-compatible endpoints from Ollama, vLLM or llama.cpp so models are swappable, and\n  tracing for production samples.\n- **What you control.** The test cases, the metrics and pass thresholds, the models and quantizations\n  compared, and the release gate.\n- **What you can skip.** Running every public benchmark. Use them only to shortlist; the decision comes from\n  your own cases.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Model selection.** Teams shortlist a few Llama, Qwen, Phi, Gemma or Mistral small models and pick one\n  from a scorecard on their own tasks.\n- **Quantization decisions.** Choosing between 4-bit and 8-bit builds for a device or GPU budget based on\n  measured task success.\n- **Fine-tuning and distillation gates.** Proving a trained adapter beats the prompted baseline before it\n  ships.\n- **Cascade tuning.** Using the same set to set escalation thresholds between an SLM and a large model.","mini_project":"**Build an SLM scorecard for a three-tool agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** rank three local models or quantizations on your own agent task with a reusable harness.\n- **Steps:** define three tool schemas; write 100 cases (each tool, \"no tool\", tricky arguments, paraphrases)\n  plus 10 multi-step sandbox tasks; pull candidates in Ollama; run each three times; compute parse rate, tool\n  and argument accuracy, task success, and median and p95 latency; categorise the top failures.\n- **Done when:** you have a scorecard table, a recommendation with a stated release threshold, and a script\n  that re-scores a new model with one config change."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    G[(\"Golden set: traces, SME and synthetic cases\")] --> H[\"Eval harness\"]\n    C[\"Candidate SLMs and quantizations\"] --> H\n    B[\"Baseline model\"] --> H\n    H --> S1[\"Call metrics: parse, schema, tool, args\"]\n    H --> S2[\"Task metrics: sandbox success, steps\"]\n    H --> S3[\"Ops metrics: latency, memory, cost\"]\n    S1 --> R{\"Meets every gate?\"}\n    S2 --> R\n    S3 --> R\n    R -->|\"yes\"| D[\"Ship behind canary\"]\n    R -->|\"no\"| F[\"Failure analysis: prompt, constrain, fine-tune\"]","caption":"Every candidate runs the same frozen cases under the same settings; only the scorecard decides."},"interview":[{"q":"Why are public benchmarks not enough to choose an SLM for an agent?","a":"- **They measure general knowledge and reasoning**, not your tool schemas, prompt format, multi-step\n  stability or refusal behaviour.\n- **They rarely test your served configuration**, and small models are sensitive to quantization, chat\n  template and decoding settings, so use benchmarks to shortlist and your own set to decide."},{"q":"How would you build an eval set for an SLM that will call five internal tools?","a":"- **Cover each tool and \"no tool\"**, argument variants, ambiguous requests, paraphrases and injected tool\n  results, sourced from traces and SME-written cases.\n- **Use code checks** for tool names, arguments and sandbox state, add a multi-step task suite, freeze and\n  version the set, and keep it out of training data."},{"q":"Your 3B model's tool accuracy is fine but end-to-end task success is low. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Errors compound:** 95% per step over many steps gives much lower task success, so inspect trajectories for\n  where they derail.\n- **Categorise failures** (looping, ignoring tool errors, losing context, wrong stop) and pick fixes: shorter\n  context, constrained output, fine-tuning on trajectories, or escalation for long tasks."},{"q":"How do you compare quantization levels fairly?","a":"- **Hold everything else fixed** (model, prompts, engine, hardware) and run the full suite at each level\n  with repeated runs.\n- **Report quality and operational metrics together**, such as task success, latency and memory, because the\n  cheapest level that still meets every threshold is the right choice."},{"q":"What release gate would you put in front of an SLM swap in production?","a":"- **Per-slice thresholds** on parse rate, schema validity, tool accuracy and task success relative to the\n  current model, run in CI on every model or prompt change.\n- **Then shadow traffic and a canary** with monitoring of parse rate, escalation rate and task success by\n  model version, with automatic rollback."}],"tradeoffs":["**Own eval set vs public benchmarks.** Your set predicts your agent's behaviour but costs time to build and maintain; public benchmarks are free but only good for shortlisting.","**Code checks vs LLM judges.** Code checks are precise and cheap but cover only verifiable outputs; judges cover free text but add cost, bias and noise that small score differences can hide.","**Big suite vs fast iteration.** Thousands of cases detect small regressions but slow every experiment; keep a fast smoke subset for development and the full suite for release gates.","**Single run vs repeated runs.** One deterministic run is cheap but hides instability; repeated sampled runs show variance at multiplied cost."],"pitfalls":["**Testing a different configuration than you serve.** Symptom: the model scores well offline and fails in production. Fix: evaluate the exact quantization, engine, chat template and decoding settings you deploy.","**Only call-level metrics.** Symptom: high tool accuracy but tasks still fail. Fix: add multi-step sandbox tasks and measure end-to-end success.","**Too few cases to separate models.** Symptom: rankings flip between runs. Fix: grow slices, repeat runs and report spread before declaring a winner.","**Leaking eval cases into fine-tuning data.** Symptom: scores jump after training, production does not. Fix: keep the golden set separate and deduplicate training data against it."],"when":"- **Use when** choosing between SLMs or quantizations, before and after fine-tuning or distillation, when\n  tuning a cascade, and on every model or prompt change.\n- **Avoid** relying on it alone when production traffic differs sharply from the set; shadow and online\n  metrics must confirm.\n- **Measure** parse rate, schema validity, tool and argument accuracy, task success, steps, latency, memory\n  and cost per successful task.\n- **Interviews:** show how you would build the set and why public benchmarks are only a shortlist.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the model-agnostic harness, writes code-checked cases for each tool and runs candidates, quantizations and fine-tunes through the same scorecard.","architect":"Defines the metrics, thresholds and release gates that every model change must pass, and ties eval results to cost and latency budgets.","fde":"Builds the golden set with the customer's experts from their real requests and uses the scorecard to justify the model choice to stakeholders."},"check":{"q":"A new small model family tops a public leaderboard. Your team wants to swap it into the agent this\nweek. What do you do before agreeing?","a":"Run it through your own eval harness with the exact quantization, engine, chat template and decoding\nsettings you would serve, against the current production model as baseline. Check call-level metrics\n(parse rate, schema validity, tool and argument accuracy) and task-level success on multi-step sandbox\ntasks, repeated over several runs to see variance. Compare latency, memory and cost per successful task on\nyour hardware. Read failures by category. If it meets every slice threshold, run it in shadow on live\ntraffic, then canary it. Check its licence and model card too, since the leaderboard says nothing about\nyour tools."},"quiz":[{"q":"An agent's steps each succeed 95% of the time, independently. Roughly what share of 10-step tasks finish with every step correct?","options":["About 95%","About 85%","About 60%","About 10%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"0.95 raised to the 10th power is about 0.6, so per-step accuracy that looks high still loses many full tasks. This is why agent evals need task-level metrics, not only call-level ones."},{"q":"Why must an SLM eval use the exact quantization and chat template you will serve?","options":["Evaluation tools cannot load unquantized models","Small-model accuracy can shift by several points with quantization and template changes","Chat templates only matter for large models","Quantization affects latency but never accuracy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Small models are sensitive to how they are served. Testing a different configuration can rank models wrongly or hide a regression that appears only in production."},{"q":"What is the best use of public leaderboards when picking an SLM for an agent?","options":["As the final decision, since they are standardised","To shortlist candidates before testing them on your own tasks","To replace the need for a golden dataset","To estimate production latency on your hardware"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Leaderboards are a cheap filter, but they do not cover your tools, formats or hardware. The decision comes from your own eval set run on the served configuration."}]}]});
